(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m2-v22"]={"id":"v22","label":"Version 22 · natural teaching notes with researchers and years","revision":"ling406-modern-m2-v22-2","scripts":{"speech1-01":[],"speech1-02":[{"text":"**Speech perception** is the process of recovering a linguistic message from the sound of speech, so listeners pull a message as clear as “I can see you” out of a signal that seems like a mess.","children":["The energy of each sound runs into the next, because the tongue, lips and other articulators in the **vocal tract** move without pause and their movements overlap."]},{"text":"Listeners also get **the same message from very different physical stimuli**, because voices and vocal tract sizes differ and yet the same word is heard.","children":["Across many speakers each vowel covers a broad range of formant values, and the ranges **overlap**, so one pair of values can be different vowels for different speakers."]},{"text":"Recognizing speech means finding a **stable linguistic identity** inside enormous physical variation.","children":[]}],"speech1-03":[{"text":"The premise is that listeners must **turn a highly variable, analog signal into something more abstract** that the language system can operate on.","children":["An **analog signal** varies continuously, so it has no built-in markers of where one speech sound stops and the next starts."]},{"text":"The abstract form is a **symbolic representation**, which identifies a unit such as the **phoneme**, a sound category that distinguishes words, and sets fine physical detail aside.","children":["/b/ in “bat” and /p/ in “pat” are different phonemes because swapping them changes the word, while many slightly different versions of /b/ count as one phoneme."]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** is thought to reflect this **“translation” from physical to symbolic**, because listeners are less sensitive to differences inside a category and more sensitive to equally large differences that cross a boundary.","children":["It is one account of the translation, and alternative ideas exist."]}],"speech1-04":[{"text":"The **vocal tract** is the passage from the larynx to the lips, and according to **Ladefoged (1996)** its shape is related in systematic ways to **which resonant frequencies get amplified**.","children":["A **resonant frequency** is one that a space naturally strengthens, so changing the shape of the vocal tract changes which frequencies get the biggest boost."]},{"text":"**Formants** are the resonant frequencies the vocal tract emphasizes, and every vowel has its own combination of them.","children":["From **heed** through hid, head and had the tongue lowers and the first two formants draw closer, and from **hod** through hawed, hood and who’d the tongue moves back, the lips round, and both formants sit low and close.","A vowel is defined by the **relationship among its resonances**, which stays similar across speakers."]},{"text":"A **formant transition** is a change in a formant’s frequency over time, which happens as the mouth moves between a consonant and a vowel.","children":["The steady part of a vowel tells about the vowel, and the moving part tells about the neighboring sound."]}],"speech1-05":[{"text":"A **spectrogram** maps acoustic energy over time and frequency, and the bands of strong energy in each vowel are its **formants**.","children":["The recordings are Rob Hagiwara’s samples of English vowels."]},{"text":"The words **bead, bid, bade, bed, bad, bod, baud, bode, “bood” and booed** all start with /b/ and end with /d/, so the differences between them come mainly from the vowels.","children":["**F1** is the lowest formant and **F2** is the next one up, and the pattern of these two carries most of what tells vowels apart."]},{"text":"In **bead**, F1 is very low and F2 is very high, and through bid, bade, bed and bad F1 rises while F2 falls, so the two move closer together.","children":["In the back rounded vowels such as **bode, “bood” and booed**, F2 drops low and sits near F1, so booed pairs a low F1 with a low F2."]}],"speech1-06":[{"text":"**Source-filter synthesis** builds speech from two parts, the **source**, which is the sound energy the voice produces, and the **filter**, which is how the vocal tract shapes that energy.","children":["Pulling them apart in **Praat**, a free program for speech analysis, makes it possible to change one while the other stays fixed."]},{"text":"The recording is a speaker saying **/u/**, the vowel in “booed,” and its **spectrogram** breaks the sound into frequency components while the **waveform** records air pressure over time.","children":[]},{"text":"This /u/ has a **low first formant** and a **very low second formant**, which fits its back rounded quality.","children":["If changing only the filter changes the vowel, then the vowel’s identity must lie in the filter, which is its pattern of formants."]}],"speech1-07":[{"text":"**Praat can find formants automatically** by following each resonance through the recording, and in this /u/ it identifies **five formants**.","children":["Several resonances run at the same time, and each one is a frequency component of the same sound."]},{"text":"**Only the first three formants are usually important for phonemic processing**, because F1, F2 and F3 carry most of the information that separates phonemes, while higher formants depend more on the individual voice.","children":[]},{"text":"The example deals with only **the first two formants**, because changing just these two is enough to turn one vowel into another.","children":["The higher formants stay fixed, so any change that listeners hear comes from F1 or F2 alone."]}],"speech1-08":[{"text":"The **source** can be **extracted** from the recorded /u/ by building a model of the filter from the measured formants and removing that filter’s effect from the recording.","children":["What is left is a **more-or-less “unfiltered” version of the voice**, and it is only more or less unfiltered because the separation is an estimate."]},{"text":"The extracted source **sounds like a buzz**, because it keeps its energy and pitch but loses the resonance pattern that made it sound like /u/.","children":["Pitch survives because it comes from vocal fold vibration, and vowel quality disappears because it comes from the filter."]},{"text":"The separation is **a little bit like doing a multiple regression analysis**, in which the contribution of one predictor is estimated and removed so that the rest can be examined.","children":["Once the source is isolated, new filters can be applied to it."]}],"speech1-09":[{"text":"Three **filters** can be applied to the same source, and each filter is a resonance pattern that specifies which frequencies get more emphasis and which get less.","children":[]},{"text":"The **first filter** is just the first four formants of the original /u/, so applying it should bring back the original vowel.","children":["The **second filter** raises **the first formant a little**, and the **third filter** raises **the second formant a lot**, while the other formants stay roughly in place."]},{"text":"The **source stays the same** for all three filters, so any change in vowel quality comes from the filter.","children":["No new speaker or recording is needed, and comparing the outputs isolates what a change in the lowest resonance does compared with a change in the second."]}],"speech1-10":[{"text":"**Multiplying the source by the filters gives new vowels**, because the output is the source shaped by the filter and each filter boosts a different set of frequencies.","children":["The source provides the raw sound energy, and the filter decides how much of that energy stays strong in each frequency region."]},{"text":"With the **original filter** the result keeps the quality of /u/, with **F1 raised a little** the vowel changes, and with **F2 raised a lot** the back rounded /u/ moves toward a more front vowel.","children":[]},{"text":"All three outputs share the same buzz, pitch and timing, yet they sound like different vowels, so **vowel quality comes from the filter**.","children":["A researcher can change vowel quality by swapping only the filter, without asking a speaker to record each version."]}],"speech1-11":[{"text":"Formant values are only **partly** set by the intended vowel, because they also depend on **anatomical features** that differ between speakers, on **rates of speech**, and on **context**. Context means the **phones**, or particular spoken sounds, before and after a vowel.","children":["A longer vocal tract gives lower resonances, fast speech gives the articulators less time to reach their targets, and the tongue and lips are already heading toward the next sound.","Across many speakers the ranges for different vowels overlap, so no single pair of frequencies defines a vowel for everyone."]},{"text":"Formant values **nonetheless** give a pretty good description of **vowel space**, the layout of a language’s vowels by their formant frequencies, especially for **a specific talker speaking carefully**.","children":["All else being equal, reasonable approximations of vowels can be made by manipulating **steady-state formants**, the resonances in the stable middle of a vowel."]}],"speech1-12":[{"text":"The **pattern playback synthesizer** turns a painted spectrogram pattern back into sound, so researchers paint bands on a transparent sheet wherever they want acoustic energy and the machine plays the pattern aloud.","children":[]},{"text":"The machine shines light through a **cylindrical lens** onto a spinning **tone wheel**, whose rings modulate the light at many frequencies at once.","children":["A lens and **mirror** send that light onto the painted **spectrogram**, where the painted areas pass along the matching frequencies.","A **light collector** and **photocell** turn the light into an electrical signal, and an **amplifier** and **loudspeaker** turn that signal into sound."]},{"text":"A researcher can change a single formant band or transition while everything else stays the same, so any change in what listeners hear can be traced to **the one painted change**.","children":["Series of patterns differing in tiny steps revealed how formant transitions signal consonants."]}],"speech1-13":[{"text":"**Stop consonants are distinguished by formant transitions**, which is sensible because the audible part of a /b/, /d/ or /g/ happens **while the jaw is opening**.","children":["During the opening the vocal tract changes shape quickly, so the formants glide from the consonant’s position toward the vowel’s position."]},{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** used the **pattern playback synthesizer** to create speech-like sounds with two formants that replicated these transitions.","children":["The lower formant was nearly the same in every stimulus, while the upper formant started at a different frequency in each stimulus and then settled at the same vowel level."]},{"text":"The patterns kept the vowel the same and changed only where the upper transition began, so the responses of listeners could be tied to the transition.","children":["A **rising** transition was heard as /b/, a roughly **flat** one as /d/ and a **falling** one as /g/."]}],"speech1-14":[{"text":"A **speech continuum** is a series of sounds that change step by step along one controlled acoustic property.","children":["Here **F1 is kept constant** and the **F2 onset frequency**, where the second formant begins its transition, is **varied linearly** from the first stimulus to the last.","Because the change is linear, **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**."]},{"text":"Along the series the F2 transition goes from rising, to nearly flat, to falling as the onset climbs past the vowel frequency.","children":[]},{"text":"Every step is physically equal, so the continuum fairly tests whether perception follows the acoustics or the categories.","children":["A purely acoustic listener would hear every step as the same size, while a categorical listener would hear steps across a boundary as different and steps within a category as alike."]}],"speech1-15":[{"text":"An **ideal observer model** predicts how a listener would respond under a particular account, and this one assumes perception is **non-categorical**, tracking the acoustic signal gradually with no sharp boundaries.","children":["It is a baseline for the real labeling data of **Liberman and colleagues (1957)**."]},{"text":"Its first prediction is that **categorization gets “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**, where a **prototype** is the clearest example of a category.","children":["The endpoints are /b/ and /g/ with /d/ in the middle, and **stimulus ambiguity scales with the distance from the prototype**.","The second stimulus would be heard as /b/ about three quarters of the time and as /d/ about one quarter of the time."]},{"text":"**Labeling** would change smoothly, with responses for one category fading out in a straight slope while the neighboring category fades in, so two labels are equally likely halfway between prototypes.","children":[]}],"speech1-16":[{"text":"The ideal observer can also assume that perception is **linear**, so equal acoustic differences are perceived as equally large, and this predicts that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":["**Labeling** asks a listener to identify a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/, while **discrimination** asks only whether two sounds are “same” or “different.”"]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a “peaked” function**, because there are three categories and listeners are choosing between labels, so each category’s responses rise toward its prototype and fall away.","children":[]},{"text":"Every discrimination pair is two steps apart with the same physical separation, so if perception of differences in F2 onset were constant over the whole continuum, **discrimination accuracy would also be constant**.","children":["A flat pattern would mean boundaries play no role in hearing differences, and a peaked pattern would mean boundaries make some differences easier to hear."]}],"speech1-17":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** had listeners label each stimulus on the continuum from /b/ through /d/ to /g/ and judge pairs that were one, two or three steps apart, and the results are **categorical perception**.","children":[]},{"text":"Labeling had **sharp changes**, because the first few stimuli were heard as /b/ almost every time and responses then switched almost completely to /d/, and later just as quickly to /g/.","children":["The narrow region where the label flips is the **category boundary**, and these switches are far more abrupt than the gradual slopes the ideal observer predicted."]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**, while pairs inside one category were discriminated poorly, close to **chance**, the level expected from guessing.","children":["Larger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries.","Discrimination predicted from labeling alone captured much of the pattern, but real accuracy was often a little higher, especially within categories, so listeners keep some sensitivity beyond the labels."]}],"speech1-18":[],"speech1-19":[{"text":"Data from students in prior years tested categorical perception along a continuum from **beach** to **peach**, two words that differ in their first consonant, /b/ or /p/.","children":["Each listener did an **identification** task, labeling every stimulus, and a **discrimination** task, judging pairs two steps apart."]},{"text":"For the first listener, identification stays on one word for the early stimuli and **switches to the other word** around the middle, with mixed responses only near the switch.","children":["That listener discriminates best for the pairs that straddle the identification boundary, and pairs well inside one category are hard to tell apart even though their physical separation is the same."]},{"text":"The second listener **switches labels later** along the continuum and discriminates best near that later boundary.","children":["For each listener, the best discrimination falls close to that person’s **own identification boundary**."]}],"speech1-20":[{"text":"**Not everyone has exactly the same category boundary**, so a pair that straddles one person’s boundary may stay inside one category for someone else.","children":["When individual boundaries differ, averaging across the group can flatten the boundary peak."]},{"text":"**In general, discrimination is best for stimuli perceived as being in different categories**, because the same size of difference is far easier to hear when the two sounds are heard as different words.","children":[]},{"text":"To make the lab doable in a short time, the discrimination experiment included **only “different” trials**, so the correct answer on every trial was “different” if the listener could discriminate the pair.","children":["Accuracy is the share of pairs heard as different, so low accuracy on within-category pairs means listeners often failed to hear differences that were really there."]}],"speech1-21":[{"text":"More results from prior years compare a continuum from **bear** to **pear** with the one from **beach** to **peach**.","children":["For bear and pear, identification switches fairly clearly, but discrimination jumps up and down from one pair to the next, including a miss on a pair that crosses the boundary.","Another listener’s data for beach and peach fluctuate even more, with identification zigzagging instead of switching once."]},{"text":"The one iron-clad law of experimental research is that **stuff happens**, so responses to a carefully built continuum can still bounce around because of noise in a few responses.","children":[]},{"text":"The experiment could have been better in two ways.","children":["Only “different” trials were used, so nothing measured how often listeners said “different” when the sounds were identical.","The short session gave each listener few trials per pair, so a single response could move accuracy a great deal."]}],"speech1-22":[{"text":"Categorical perception helps by giving **stable word recognition**, even though being worse at hearing differences within a category seems like throwing away useful detail.","children":["Every production of a phoneme differs physically from every other, because of speakers, speaking rates and neighboring sounds.","If each small acoustic change were heard as a new identity, everyday versions of one word would sound like unrelated sounds."]},{"text":"Reduced sensitivity within a category lets many acoustic versions count as **one phoneme**, and that variation is tolerated because it leaves the word unchanged.","children":[]},{"text":"Differences **between categories** stay easy to hear, so the distinctions that change words are kept and the listener is free to focus on the differences that change meaning.","children":[]}],"speech1-23":[],"speech1-24":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** can be tested even though infants cannot name sounds, because infants react to novelty and researchers can watch for a behavior that changes when an infant notices something new.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, from Janet Werker’s lab, an infant learns to turn the head when a sound changes and earns a visual reward such as a moving toy.","children":["After the infant learns this link, a head turn following a change means the infant detected it."]},{"text":"The results of interest come from **high-amplitude sucking**, in which the infant sucks on a pacifier and strong sucks make a sound play.","children":["As the same sound repeats the infant gets used to it, which is **habituation**, and sucking slows.","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has noticed the change."]}],"speech1-25":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** tested categorical perception in **four-month-old infants** with **high-amplitude sucking**, using sounds that varied in **voice onset time (VOT)**, the delay between the release of a consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration.","children":["Adults hear a short VOT as a voiced consonant such as /b/ and a longer VOT as a voiceless consonant such as /p/."]},{"text":"Infants heard one syllable until sucking slowed, and then the VOT changed by the same amount in two conditions or did not change in a control condition.","children":["In one condition the change **crossed** the adult category boundary, and in the other both sounds came from the **same** adult category."]},{"text":"Sucking **recovered** sharply after the change across the boundary, but it kept falling after the change within a category, much like the control group.","children":["The two changes were equal in size, so the difference comes from crossing the boundary, and infants **perceive voicing categorically** before they can produce or label speech."]}],"speech1-26":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing** is the process in which experience tunes perception to the sounds of the surrounding language while sensitivity to some distinctions from other languages declines.","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested English-learning infants at three ages on two **non-English contrasts**, a **Hindi** contrast between two “ta”-like sounds and a **Salish** contrast between “ki” and “qi.”","children":["At six to eight months most infants reached the discrimination criterion on both contrasts, at eight to ten months fewer did, and at ten to twelve months very few did.","Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants succeeded nearly every time on the contrast from their own language."]},{"text":"These results are described as **perceptual reorganization during the first year of life**.","children":["Infants who hear a contrast in their own language keep it, so the decline depends on **language experience**."]}],"speech1-27":[{"text":"A **very compelling narrative** links these findings in three claims.","children":["First, speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities that are a special property of the language system**.","Second, these abilities are **present very early in development**, as the infant sucking and head turn experiments suggest."]},{"text":"Third, children **start off as “citizens of the world,”** able to hear **all** contrasts, and then **rapidly tune their perceptual systems to their specific language**.","children":["Tuning means that contrasts of the surrounding language stay available while contrasts from other languages fade, as in the Werker and Tees (1984) results."]},{"text":"The narrative proposes broad, language-specific abilities at birth and treats development mostly as narrowing from that broad start.","children":[]}],"speech1-28":[{"text":"The compelling narrative meets **some issues**, and three of them concern **categorical perception**.","children":["It is **not limited to language**, because it also appears with nonspeech sounds, and it is **not limited to humans**, because nonhuman animals also perceive speech sounds categorically.","It is **task-dependent**, because choosing between labels makes responses more categorical than a task that measures fine acoustic differences."]},{"text":"**Infant speech perception** is **not actually “universal,”** because some contrasts are harder for infants to detect than others from early on.","children":[]},{"text":"Together these issues weaken the strong claim that every speech distinction starts out equally available in a mechanism devoted only to language.","children":[]}],"speech1-29":[{"text":"**Cutting and Rosner (1974)** tested categorical perception with **non-speech sounds** that varied in **rise time**, which is how quickly a sound reaches full loudness after it begins.","children":["A **rapid rise time** sounds **plucked**, like a plucked string, and a **slow rise time** sounds **bowed**, like a bowed string.","The stimuli included sawtooth waves, sine waves and speech syllables, each with short and long rise times."]},{"text":"For the sawtooth waves, listeners labeled short rise times as pluck and long rise times as bow, and the label switched over a narrow range in the middle of the continuum.","children":["Discrimination was only a little above chance for most pairs, but it **peaked** right where the labels switched."]},{"text":"The link between a label boundary and better discrimination appears for a sound distinction that has **nothing to do with language**.","children":[]}],"speech1-30":[{"text":"**Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** tested whether **infants** have categorical perception of **non-speech sounds**, using high-amplitude sucking to measure whether a change was detected.","children":["Infants heard one sound repeatedly until sucking declined, and then the sound was shifted in one of three conditions, **no change**, a change **within categories** or a change **between categories**."]},{"text":"After no change and after a within-category change, sucking stayed low, but after a between-category change it jumped back up and then declined again as the new sound became familiar.","children":["This mirrors the pattern that Eimas and colleagues (1971) found for speech."]},{"text":"Infants cannot be drawing on knowledge of words or phonemes when they respond to plucked and bowed sounds, so their categorical response must come from **more general auditory processing**, which could also underlie their response to speech.","children":[]}],"speech1-31":[{"text":"**Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)**, following earlier work by **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**, found categorical perception of speech sounds in **chinchillas**.","children":["The animals were trained with **operant conditioning**, learning in which consequences change how often a behavior occurs, that either the zero-lag stimulus or the long-lag stimulus was an **SD**, a **discriminative stimulus** signaling that a response will earn food.","The lag is **voice onset time**, and humans hear the zero lag as /ga/ and the long lag as /ka/."]},{"text":"When tested on sounds in between, chinchillas labeled short VOTs as /ga/ almost every time and long VOTs almost never, and the switch had the **same steep, categorical shape** as in human listeners.","children":["A louder **burst**, the brief noise at the release of a stop, and louder **aspiration noise**, the breathy noise after a voiceless release, shifted the boundary toward shorter VOTs or made the switch steeper, for one chinchilla and one human."]},{"text":"Animals without human language can sort a speech continuum into categories, which argues against the view that categorical perception is special to human language.","children":[]}],"speech1-32":[{"text":"Some contrasts are **less salient**, meaning harder to notice, than others for infants.","children":["The syllables **[ma]**, **[na]** and **[ŋa]** differ in **place of articulation**, which is where in the mouth the sound is made, and [ŋa] begins with the sound at the end of “sing.”"]},{"text":"The **looking time** of infants was compared on same trials and change trials, and longer looking on change trials means the infant noticed the change.","children":["English-hearing infants at four to five months looked longer on change trials for the native contrast between [ma] and [na], but not for the nonnative contrast between [na] and [ŋa].","**Filipino-learning infants**, who hear [na] and [ŋa] as a native contrast, did not detect the change at six to eight months but did by ten to twelve months."]},{"text":"Experience **strengthens** sensitivity to some native contrasts, so development includes gains as well as losses.","children":[]}],"speech2-01":[],"speech2-02":[{"text":"**Klatt (1979)** described a very old and very general model of speech perception, in which the **speech waveform** passes through a series of stages. The stages run from **spectral analysis** in the cochlea, through **acoustic property detectors** and **phonetic feature detectors**, to **segmental analysis** and **lexical search**.","children":["Lexical search compares the sequence of segments with stored words and produces **lexical hypotheses**, which are candidate words that might match."]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** fits this model, because **information is lost** somewhere between the analog signal from the cochlea and the activation of phonemes.","children":["This **loss is helpful**, because a phoneme sequence can make contact with higher levels, so it seems that identifying words requires **first identifying their constituent phonemes**."]},{"text":"Two problems challenge this simple view, which are **cascaded processing**, where partial information moves on before a decision is complete, and **feedback**, where a higher level such as words influences a lower level such as phonemes.","children":[]}],"speech2-03":[{"text":"The **beach-peach continuum** is a familiar **voicing continuum**, in which the first consonant changes from /p/ to /b/ as **voice onset time (VOT)** gets shorter in equal steps.","children":["VOT is the delay between the release of the lips and the start of vocal fold vibration, so a long delay sounds like peach and a short delay sounds like beach."]},{"text":"One listener's **identification** responses are **step-like**, because the early stimuli get one word every time, the late stimuli get the other word every time, and only one stimulus near the middle gets mixed labels.","children":["The **category boundary** is the narrow region where the preferred label switches."]},{"text":"The same listener's **discrimination** follows the boundary, because pairs inside one category are almost never heard as different while pairs that **straddle the boundary** are heard as different every time.","children":["Equal changes in VOT are much easier to detect when they change the word than when the word stays the same."]}],"speech2-04":[{"text":"A **threshold model** is one answer to how categorical perception happens, because in it **activity is only passed forward from one level to the next when an activity threshold is crossed**.","children":["A **threshold** is a set level of activity that must be reached, so activity below it can rise and fall with no effect on the next level."]},{"text":"In this kind of model, **categorical perception arises at phoneme identification**.","children":["Voicing evidence at the feature level swings between a **+voiced** threshold and a **-voiced** threshold while /b/ and /p/ both stay silent, and only when the evidence crosses the +voiced threshold does **/b/** switch on **all at once**."]},{"text":"An ambiguous sound either crosses a threshold or does not, so the word level receives a finished /b/ or a finished /p/.","children":["Two different sounds that both end up as /b/ become **identical** by the time they reach word recognition, because the earlier uncertainty stays behind at the feature level."]}],"speech2-05":[{"text":"A **cascaded model** gives a different answer, because in it **activity is passed forward continuously over time from one level to the next**, while the decision is still forming.","children":["Feature activity and phoneme activity rise and fall together, so growing +voiced evidence raises **/b/** and a shift toward -voiced moves activation toward **/p/**."]},{"text":"In this kind of model, **categorical perception might happen later on, or might even be an artifact of the task structure**, where an **artifact** is a result created by the way something is measured.","children":["An identification task forces a click on beach or peach even if both words were partly active, so the answer looks categorical because the **choices are categorical**."]},{"text":"Mixed voicing evidence gives /b/ and /p/ activation in proportion to their evidence, so a sound near the boundary **partly activates both beach and peach**.","children":["Two sounds labeled /b/ can still differ in how much they supported /p/ along the way."]}],"speech2-06":[{"text":"**Making furniture** is a metaphor for **thresholded activity**, because each operation turns one specific input into one specific output.","children":["A **lumber mill** turns **trees** into **lumber**, a **furniture factory** turns lumber into **unassembled furniture**, and an **IKEA customer** turns unassembled furniture into finished **furniture**."]},{"text":"Each step needs a **finished product** in a form it can use, so trees must become lumber before furniture is made, and lumber must become parts before assembly starts.","children":[]},{"text":"**Thresholded processing** depends on its inputs in the same way, because a higher level **waits** until the lower level delivers the required representation.","children":["Information moves forward only as a completed product."]}],"speech2-07":[{"text":"In the furniture metaphor, each **“layer”** of the system **can only take a specific input that has been transformed completely by the previous layer**, so manufacturing waits for the shipment of lumber.","children":["**Many computer programs** function this way, with each stage handing on only its finished output while the higher stage sits idle."]},{"text":"In speech recognition, a strict threshold account makes **word processing depend on an identified phoneme**, so a partly resolved acoustic signal stays at the lower level and the word level receives only the finished segment.","children":[]},{"text":"This design also decides **what information is lost**, because different sounds that become the same phoneme never pass their earlier differences to word recognition.","children":["That helps treat varied sounds alike, but later stages never learn **how uncertain** the earlier stages were."]}],"speech2-08":[{"text":"A **food web** is a metaphor for **cascaded processing**, because its **“levels” continuously interact with one another**.","children":["A drought that reduces the number of desert plants **immediately** affects the health of kangaroo rats, which affects the snakes that eat them, and no population waits for the level below to reach a finished state."]},{"text":"**Cascaded processing** has the same continuous dependence, because shifting feature evidence changes phoneme activity as it happens, and shifting phoneme activity changes support for words.","children":[]},{"text":"The food web also has clear **feedback loops**, because adding more hawks changes the populations of snakes, lizards and rats, and those changes in turn affect the hawks' own food supply.","children":["In a recognition model, **feedback** is influence from a higher level back down to a lower level, while cascading sends developing evidence forward."]}],"speech2-09":[{"text":"**Phoneme identification for word recognition** could be more like **thresholded processing**, the lumber mill, or **cascaded processing**, the food web, and the eye-movement evidence of **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** favors cascaded processing.","children":["In a thresholded account, word recognition starts only once a phoneme decision is complete, while in a cascaded account a word can gather support while the phoneme is still uncertain."]},{"text":"Two sounds can both be labeled **/b/** and still differ in how much they resemble **/p/**.","children":["A thresholded system turns both into the same finished /b/, so **pear should not compete with bear**.","A cascaded system passes along more /p/ support for the more ambiguous sound, so **pear should compete with bear for a while**."]},{"text":"Both accounts can predict the same final word, so the deciding test measures **temporary support for a competing word** during recognition.","children":[]}],"speech2-10":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** tested whether phoneme identification is thresholded or cascaded by looking for **gradient effects of within-category VOT on lexical access**.","children":["**Lexical access** is retrieving a word from memory, **gradient** means changing by degrees, and **within-category VOT** means VOT differences among sounds heard as the same phoneme."]},{"text":"Listeners identified sounds from a **/b/-/p/ continuum** in a **“visual world” display** while **eye-tracking** recorded where they looked among the pictures.","children":["Each display had a **target** such as pear, a **competitor** such as bear that differs only in its first consonant, and two unrelated **fillers**, a lamp and a ship."]},{"text":"Eye movements are fast and continuous, and listeners look at objects as they think about them, so looks to the competitor measure **how active the competitor was** before the final click.","children":[]}],"speech2-11":[{"text":"In the study by **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)**, participants used the **mouse to select** whichever of the pictures was most consistent with what they heard, such as a **“bear”** or a **“pear.”**","children":["The click records the word the listener finally settled on."]},{"text":"The clicks produced a **typical, step-like identification function**, because short VOTs were almost never heard as pear, long VOTs almost always were, and the switch happened abruptly at a **category boundary**.","children":[]},{"text":"Judged by the clicks alone, perception looks **fully categorical**, which on its own would seem to support the threshold model.","children":["A click records only the word chosen at the end, while eye movements record which other words received support before that choice.","A sharp identification function can therefore exist alongside gradual differences in competition."]}],"speech2-12":[{"text":"In the study by **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)**, **eye movements reveal that ambiguity is gradient**, which means that uncertainty varies by degree, because there are **more looks to the competitor close to the boundary**.","children":["The analysis holds the final response constant by comparing only trials that ended with the same click, so differences in competitor looks cannot come from listeners changing their answer."]},{"text":"On trials where listeners clicked **bear**, sounds closer to the boundary drew **more and longer looks to the pear** than the clearest /b/ sounds.","children":["On trials where listeners clicked **pear**, the pattern was the mirror image, with the most looks to the bear for sounds just past the boundary."]},{"text":"The effect of VOT is mainly on the **duration of activation**, meaning how long the competitor stays active.","children":["The recognition system keeps track of how ambiguous each sound was, which is information a **threshold model would throw away**."]}],"speech2-13":[{"text":"The gradient effect found by **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** is clearer when competitor looks are compared across the **whole VOT range**.","children":["The measure is looks to the pear on the side of the **category boundary** where listeners clicked bear, and looks to the bear on the side where they clicked pear."]},{"text":"In both an **early** and a **late** time window after the word begins, competitor looks **increase as VOT approaches the boundary from either side**.","children":["Looks to the pear grow steadily toward the boundary on the bear side, and looks to the bear fall steadily away from the boundary on the pear side.","Early looks are higher overall, but they follow the same pattern."]},{"text":"The effect appears in both windows, so uncertainty about the first sound shapes looking **throughout recognition**, and rival support is stronger the closer the sound is to the boundary.","children":[]}],"speech2-14":[{"text":"The eye-movement finding of **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** is consistent with the idea that **activation flows in a cascaded way from features to phonemes**.","children":["**Even stimuli that are always identified as /b/ can vary in how strongly and unambiguously the “voiced” feature is activated**, so a /b/ near the boundary activates voicing only weakly."]},{"text":"/b/ and /p/ are both **bilabial**, with the closure made by the two lips, and both **stops**, with airflow fully blocked and then released.","children":["A stimulus that is obviously **+bilabial** and **+stop** supports both equally, so weak voicing evidence creates **momentary ambiguity between /b/ and /p/**, which is reflected in the eye movements while the participant decides where to click."]},{"text":"The **TRACE** model captures this cascade with a **feature layer**, a **phoneme layer** and a **word layer**.","children":["The feature layer takes a **pseudo-spectral input representation**, a simplified version of the frequency content of the sound over time, and has detectors for power, vocalic, diffuseness, acuteness, gradualness of onset, voicing and burst.","Activity passes up from features to phonemes and from phonemes to words, and units within a layer compete through **inhibition**."]}],"speech2-15":[{"text":"**Andruski and colleagues (1994)** found that **semantic priming**, the speed-up in recognizing a word after hearing a word related in meaning, can happen even with **nonwords**, so **partial activation can reach all the way to the lexical level**.","children":["**“Beace”** primes **“war”** and **“ging”** primes **“queen,”** because they are peace and king with a voiced first consonant.","In **word-to-word** items the altered word is another real word, as when **“bill,”** an altered form of pill, primes **“drug.”**"]},{"text":"Participants did a **lexical decision task**, deciding as quickly as possible whether a target is a real word, after primes that were **unaltered**, had VOT reduced by **one third** or by **two thirds**, or were **unrelated**. Every related prime sped up responses compared with unrelated primes.","children":["Word-to-nonword priming stayed strong at every level of alteration, while word-to-word priming weakened at two thirds, because the altered prime also partly activates a competing real word such as bill."]},{"text":"**Lexical representations can be activated even by incomplete or imperfect input**, because beace partly activates peace and that activation spreads to meaning, which fits cascaded processing.","children":[]}],"speech2-16":[{"text":"The bear and pear eye-tracking result of **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** is one piece of evidence for **cascaded processing**. A thresholded model can hardly explain why **the number of looks to the other member of the minimal pair increases gradually as you approach the category boundary**.","children":["A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in a single sound, such as bear and pear."]},{"text":"In a thresholded model, graded feature activity **never reaches the phoneme level**, so every token placed in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level.","children":["Word-level competition would then be identical for every /b/, which leaves nothing to produce gradually changing competitor looks."]},{"text":"A cascaded account explains the effect directly, because more support for the competing phoneme passes on more support for its word.","children":[]}],"speech2-17":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model also includes **feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer**, so activity runs up from phonemes to words and back down from words to phonemes.","children":["**Lexical feedback** means that an active word sends support back to its own phonemes, so a word that an uncertain sound partly activates can strengthen the reading of that sound."]},{"text":"Influence therefore travels **in both directions**, because acoustic evidence changes which words are active and active words shift the balance between possible phonemes.","children":[]},{"text":"**Feedback is different from cascading**, because cascading sends incomplete evidence forward while feedback sends influence from words back down to phonemes.","children":["An interactive model can have both, and experiments in which **word knowledge changes how sounds are heard**, such as the **Ganong effect**, are the reason to add feedback."]}],"speech2-18":[{"text":"**Ganong (1980)** looked at how **phoneme identification is affected by word knowledge**, which is the source of **the Ganong effect**.","children":["The phrase “Gisses and kifts for Valentine’s day” plays on the effect by swapping the voicing of the first consonants in “kisses and gifts.”"]},{"text":"He came up with **word/nonword pairs** such as **“corpse/gorpse”** and produced continua that crossed the relevant phoneme boundary in small steps of VOT.","children":["Hearing /k/ gives the real word corpse and hearing /g/ gives the nonword gorpse, while the rest of the word stays the same at every step."]},{"text":"The design pairs **uncertain phonetic evidence** with a **difference in lexical status**, so if word knowledge affects identification, an ambiguous first consonant should more often be heard as the **sound that makes a word**.","children":["The crucial test is in the **ambiguous middle** of the continuum, where word knowledge has the most room to act."]}],"speech2-19":[{"text":"One prediction for the experiment of **Ganong (1980)** comes from a **late “correction” process**, in which **categorical perception functions as usual, but there is a bias to report having heard a word**.","children":["The listener first categorizes the sound with no help from word knowledge and then leans toward the real word at the response stage."]},{"text":"This account predicts a **general reporting bias** across **word-nonword** continua, where the voiced end forms a word, **nonword-word** continua, where the voiceless end forms a word, and **neutral** continua.","children":["A bias added after perception pushes responses toward the word by the **same amount everywhere**, so even a clearly voiceless sound would sometimes be reported as voiced when the voiced version makes a word."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect**, which is the difference in voiced responses between the two lexical conditions, is therefore the **same size at every step** of the continuum.","children":[]}],"speech2-20":[{"text":"A second possibility is that **word knowledge shifts the categorical boundary**, so it changes where the listener switches from one phoneme category to the other.","children":["Near an ambiguous onset the interpretation that forms a real word gets extra support, which changes categorization itself and not only the reported answer."]},{"text":"This account predicts that the lexical conditions differ **only in the middle** of the continuum, because at both ends the sound is clear and every condition gives the same answer.","children":[]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** should form a **peak**, since it is **strongest for the items closest to the category boundary**, where lexical information can help resolve some ambiguity about the initial phoneme.","children":["A **flat** effect points to a reporting bias, while a **peaked** effect points to a shift in the boundary itself."]}],"speech2-21":[{"text":"The results of **Ganong (1980)** are strikingly consistent with the **second model**, the **boundary-shift** account, because the **lexical effect** is small at the endpoints and largest near the boundary.","children":["A peak at the boundary **rules out a simple reporting bias**, because word knowledge matters most where the acoustic evidence is weakest."]},{"text":"Ganong raised a number of possibilities for how this could happen, focusing on different kinds of **guessing strategies**, which would let listeners use word knowledge only when unsure of the sound.","children":[]},{"text":"Later models would emphasize the potential role of **feedback** in these shifts.","children":["In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes, so the whole word can shape how an ambiguous part of it is interpreted."]}],"speech2-22":[{"text":"A **simulation of the Ganong effect in the TRACE model** tracks **phoneme and word activations** as the input unfolds. The input starts with a sound **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/** and continues with /l/, the vowel /ʌ/ and /g/, which together make **plug**.","children":["When only the first sound has arrived, /b/ and /p/ are both weakly active at about the same level, and word activation is very low."]},{"text":"As /l/ and the vowel arrive, several candidates gain activation together, including **plug**, **blood** and **blush**, because all of them fit the beginning heard so far.","children":[]},{"text":"When the final **/g/** arrives, it fits plug but not the endings of blood or blush, so **plug** becomes the most active word by a wide margin.","children":["At the phoneme level, **/p/ ends up more active than /b/**."]}],"speech2-23":[{"text":"As the input unfolds, **activity accumulates for “plug,”** especially when the **final /g/** arrives, because /g/ is highly inconsistent with **“blood”** and **“blush,”** which had also become active.","children":[]},{"text":"The TRACE model explains **word effects on phoneme category boundaries** in terms of **top-down processing**, which means that higher-level knowledge influences lower-level analysis.","children":["**Activity from the lexical level produces feedback to the phoneme level**, which gives **greater activation to the phoneme consistent with a word**, so /p/ gains extra support from plug."]},{"text":"The ambiguous beginning becomes clearer because /p/ belongs to the word that the whole sequence supports.","children":["Information that arrives at the end of a word can reach back and shape how an earlier sound is heard."]}],"speech2-24":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model is a version of the **Interactive Activation model** for **written word recognition**, from **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)**, and many of the principles are the same.","children":["Feature units connect to letter units and letter units connect to word units such as **WEAR, WORK, WORD** and **WEAK**, with support between compatible units, inhibition within a level, and support from active words back down to their letters."]},{"text":"Suppose a reader sees W, O and R clearly, but ink blots hide part of a final letter that fits both **R** and **K**. Only **WORK** fits, so WORK sends support back to K and the letter is perceived as K.","children":["This is the same way plug supports /p/ in speech."]},{"text":"A key issue in speech perception is that **the stimuli unfold over time**, so TRACE repeats feature, phoneme and word units at every point in time.","children":[]}],"speech2-25":[{"text":"**Categorical perception** still holds, because **given the same physical difference, it is more difficult to distinguish stimuli from the same category than from different categories**.","children":[]},{"text":"Two findings add to it at the **lexical-phonetic interface**, where words and sounds meet.","children":["**Ambiguity about phoneme identity does have an impact on word recognition**, because sounds with the same label differ in how strongly they activate a competing word.","**Word-level information seems to have an impact on phoneme categorization**, because in the Ganong effect word knowledge moves the category boundary."]},{"text":"It therefore **cannot be the case that the word recognition system just sits there waiting for the phoneme recognition system to finish doing its job**, like a lumber mill.","children":["**Spoken word recognition is more interactive**, because partial information moves forward and active words send support back down while uncertainty is still being resolved."]}],"speech2-26":[],"speech2-27":[{"text":"**Spoken word recognition is also very fast**, as **Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found with a visual world eye-tracking task in which participants followed spoken instructions to move objects shown in a display.","children":["The display had a **beaker** as the **referent**, a **beetle** as a **cohort competitor** that shares the beginning of the word, a **speaker** as a **rhyme competitor** that shares the ending, and an **unrelated** carriage."]},{"text":"Looks to the beaker and the beetle **rose together early**, because both fit the first sounds, and looks to the beetle dropped once the sounds that separate the two words arrived.","children":["Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed smaller, and looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"Looks to the target began rising **before the word had finished**, so recognition begins while the word is still unfolding, and candidates drop out as soon as they stop matching.","children":[]}],"speech2-28":[{"text":"The eye-tracking data can be compared with a **TRACE simulation** of the same task, in which the word activations of the model over time were converted into predicted looks.","children":["The predictions **closely match** the real looks for the **referent**, for the **cohort** competitor with its early rise and decline, and for the **rhyme** competitor with its smaller and later rise."]},{"text":"The **granularity** of the simulation is fine, which means it predicts activity moment by moment, and **eye movements give us an idea about the changes in “activity” for different competitors over time**.","children":["That makes it possible to **see cognitive processing before a final response is issued**."]},{"text":"This parallels the **graded effects of category goodness** in the categorical perception task, where better and worse examples of a category produce different amounts of competitor activity even when the final label is the same.","children":[]}],"sentence-01":[],"sentence-02":[{"text":"**“The horse raced past the barn fell”** is grammatical, but it feels broken because most readers first take **raced** as the **main verb**, which leaves no room for **fell** when it arrives.","children":["The sentence describes **two events**, since someone raced the horse past the barn and the horse then fell."]},{"text":"Putting back the missing words **disambiguates** it, as in **“The horse that was raced past the barn fell,”** because “that was” makes “raced past the barn” a description of which horse is meant and leaves fell as the main verb.","children":["It could fairly be called the sentence that **launched a thousand experiments**, because researchers have used it again and again to study how readers build structure as words come in."]}],"sentence-03":[{"text":"**“The horse raced past the barn fell”** is almost as famous as **“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously,”** which is grammatical but meaningless, while the horse sentence is grammatical and meaningful but **very hard to process**.","children":["A comic jokes that nobody would ever say it, and the reply is that farmers might if their horses kept falling over, which only works because the sentence really is grammatical."]},{"text":"It is the classic case of the **“garden path” effect**, in which a sentence steers the reader into one reading early on and later words reveal that the reading was wrong.","children":["Raced looks like the main verb and fell then clashes with that structure, so the reader must go back and treat “raced past the barn” as “that was raced past the barn.”","Garden paths arise because readers build structure word by word and commit to one analysis before the sentence is over."]},{"text":"So **grammaticality and processing difficulty are different things**, because a sentence with an acceptable structure can still be very hard to follow as it unfolds.","children":[]}],"sentence-04":[{"text":"**Syntactic explanations** of the garden path effect start from a **parser**, a mental process that builds a **syntactic tree** recording how the words of a sentence group into phrases and how the phrases relate.","children":[]},{"text":"In the correct tree for the horse sentence, the subject is a large noun phrase, or **NP**, holding “the horse” plus a reduced clause meaning “that was raced past the barn,” and the main verb phrase, or **VP**, contains only **fell**.","children":["In the failed parse, “raced past the barn” is the main VP with raced as the main verb, so fell has nowhere to attach and the parse breaks down."]},{"text":"The parser runs in a cognitive system with **limited memory** and **time constraints**, so it cannot wait for the end of the sentence before building structure, and it can keep only a limited number of structures in play at once.","children":[]}],"sentence-05":[{"text":"The **grammatical version** of the horse sentence is much more complex syntactically than the failed parse because it has **many more nodes**. A **node** is a point in a syntactic tree that stands for a grammatical grouping such as an NP or a VP.","children":["The correct tree needs extra nodes for the embedded clause and the silent “that” and “was,” while the failed parse only makes “raced past the barn” the main VP."]},{"text":"One way to save space is to **initially prefer smaller trees**, a strategy called **“minimal attachment,”** in which each new word is fitted into the existing structure with the fewest new nodes.","children":["Minimal attachment favors **raced as the main verb** because that analysis needs the fewest nodes, so when fell arrives and requires the larger structure, the reader has to rebuild the tree."]}],"sentence-06":[{"text":"**“Late closure”** is another parsing strategy in this framework, and it is a bias to **keep the current clause “open” as long as possible**, so incoming words are attached to the phrase being built.","children":[]},{"text":"In **“The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite,”** the phrase “with the dynamite” wants to associate with the big **verb phrase** starting with “blew open,” and **Frazier (1987)** contrasts two structures for it.","children":["The **preferred** structure attaches the phrase to the VP, so the dynamite is the **instrument** used to blow open the safe.","The **unpreferred** structure attaches it to the NP “the safe,” so the phrase says which safe was blown open."]},{"text":"Both attachments are grammatical, and here the preferred one is also the minimal attachment and the sensible meaning, so readers reach the right reading easily.","children":[]}],"sentence-07":[{"text":"Late closure is visible in a sentence that **requires the “unpreferred” parse to be sensible**, such as **“The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels.”**","children":["Jewels are a poor tool for blowing open a safe, so the sensible meaning is the safe that contained the jewels, which needs the unpreferred attachment to the noun phrase “the safe.”"]},{"text":"In careful tests of **reading time** and **eye movements**, people are **slower to read sentences where the sensible reading requires the unpreferred parse**, and the extra time appears right where the parse has to change.","children":["Readers **momentarily entertain the idea that the jewels were used to blow open the safe**, because the parser first attaches the phrase to the verb phrase. They then have to **retrace their steps** and attach it to the noun phrase."]}],"sentence-08":[{"text":"This explanation rests on a **parser that deals exclusively with the abstract syntactic structure** of the sentence, much like the **“lumber mill”** example from speech perception. In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and hands a finished product to the next stage.","children":["The parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone, without checking meaning or context, and meaning is worked out afterward."]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**, because it wants the **simplest tree possible**, which is **minimal attachment**, and it gets there partly by leaving clauses open as long as possible, which is **late closure**.","children":[]},{"text":"This deals with ambiguity very efficiently by **ignoring it until it becomes a problem**, since the parser builds only one likely structure and is usually right.","children":["When a later word does not fit, the structure must be revised, and that revision is the garden path difficulty."]}],"sentence-09":[{"text":"A **purely syntactic explanation** has a problem, because **syntactically “difficult” sentences are not always difficult to parse**.","children":["Published research articles are full of **reduced relative clauses**, which are relative clauses with “that was” or “that were” left out, as in “the horse raced past the barn fell.”"]},{"text":"The capitalized verbs in sentences such as “Referential information PROVIDED by the discourse is of no help,” from **Britt, Perfetti, Garrod and Rayner (1992)**, and “Recent research REPORTED by FERREIRA AND CLIFTON,” from **Frazier and Rayner (1987)**, each introduce a reduced relative, **but these are not impossibly difficult sentences**.","children":["The same holds for examples from **Trueswell, Tanenhaus and Garnsey (1994)** and **Altmann and Steedman (1988)**, which readers follow with no garden path."]},{"text":"**MacDonald, Perlmutter and Seidenberg (1994)** argued instead that **semantic factors and statistical regularities interact with parsing from very early on**.","children":["Semantic factors concern meaning, and statistical regularities are patterns in how often words occur in particular structures, so familiar words can favor a reduced relative reading from the start."]}],"sentence-10":[{"text":"The collection of reduced relatives is **wonderfully passive-aggressive**, because **many of the examples are drawn from papers that use the difficulty of parsing reduced relatives as evidence for an autonomous parser**. An **autonomous parser** builds syntactic structure without help from meaning or context.","children":["Those authors argued that reduced relatives are hard to process, yet they wrote them in their own articles and readers understood them without trouble."]},{"text":"In “the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED to a sentence,” assigned modifies analysis, and in “A person PRESENTED with the evidence EXAMINED by the lawyer,” from **Ferreira and Clifton (1986)**, presented modifies person and examined modifies evidence.","children":[]},{"text":"These were **not the only reduced relatives** in those articles, since they turned up at about **one per journal page**.","children":["Reduced relatives are routine in scientific writing, so in a supportive context a formally complex structure is easy to understand."]}],"sentence-11":[{"text":"When we read **“Recent research reported by…,”** several factors besides syntax are at work.","children":["By **semantics**, research is **inanimate**, so it is an unlikely subject for a verb like report and is easily taken as the thing that was reported.","By **lexical statistics**, the learned patterns of how often words appear in particular structures, “reported” occurs frequently in reduced relatives, and by **“world knowledge”** it does so very frequently in scientific papers, especially after the word “research.”"]},{"text":"These and **many other factors interact with the syntax of the sentence very early on**, so they help decide which structure is chosen in the first place.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The burglar arrested in the parking lot was charged today”** is easy because burglars tend to be arrested, so arrested is read as starting a reduced relative.","children":["**“The policeman arrested in the parking lot was charged today”** is harder because policemen tend to do the arresting, so arrested is first taken as the main verb and “was charged” causes a garden path, even though the grammar is identical."]}],"sentence-12":[{"text":"**“World knowledge” can be very local**, because it can come from the visual scene in front of us. **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** tested this with a **visual world** of real objects, which were a pencil, a box, an empty towel and an apple sitting on a towel.","children":["People heard **“Put the apple on the towel in the box”** and carried it out while their eye movements were recorded."]},{"text":"With **minimal attachment**, “the towel” temporarily seems to end the clause “put the apple on the towel,” so “on the towel” is taken as the **destination** when it really says **which apple** to move.","children":[]},{"text":"People **look at the empty towel while planning their movements**, as though considering it as a target, before planning a movement to the box.","children":["With the unambiguous instruction **“Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box,”** the eyes go from the apple straight to the box."]}],"sentence-13":[{"text":"In a **two-referent context** the display holds **two apples**, one on a towel and one elsewhere, along with the empty towel and the box.","children":["“The apple” alone no longer says which apple to move, so **“on the towel”** now does useful work by picking out one of the two."]},{"text":"Participants usually look at the apple on the towel and then at the box, and looks to the empty towel become much less common.","children":["**The sequence and the timing of eye movements**, measured against the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between the ambiguous and the unambiguous instructions."]},{"text":"Listeners take “on the towel” as a description of the apple from the start, so the destination reading never takes hold, and the same sentence gets a different grammatical analysis because the visual context differs.","children":[]}],"sentence-14":[{"text":"With **two apples** in the display, it suddenly becomes **felicitous**, meaning natural and appropriate, to give more information about **which apple** is meant.","children":["With one apple there is no need to say which apple, so “on the towel” seems like extra information and is easily taken as a destination."]},{"text":"So **people do not look at the empty towel as much**.","children":["With one apple, the ambiguous instruction drew looks to the empty towel on a little over half of the trials, and with two apples it did so on far fewer trials, close to the level for the unambiguous instruction."]},{"text":"The words of the instruction were the same in both displays, so this is an effect of **referential context**, the set of objects a phrase could refer to. Referential context influences the grammatical relationship listeners assign while the sentence is still being heard.","children":[]}],"sentence-15":[{"text":"The results from **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** are **a big problem for purely syntactic accounts**, because **different parses of the same sentence are favored under different semantic and pragmatic conditions, and this effect happens very early**.","children":["**Semantic** conditions concern meaning, and **pragmatic** conditions concern how language is used in a situation, such as whether a speaker must specify which object is meant."]},{"text":"The same ambiguous sentence misleads listeners often with one apple and rarely with two, so only the visual situation changes the parse the listener adopts first.","children":[]},{"text":"The difference appears in **eye movements during the sentence**, before the action is carried out.","children":["If context only repaired the parse afterward, listeners in both displays would first look at the empty towel, so the drop with two apples means context shaped the **first parse**."]}],"sentence-16":[{"text":"More recently, researchers have used **mouse movements** to study these questions, and **Farmer and colleagues (2007)** tracked the path of the computer mouse while participants carried out spoken instructions in a version of the apple and towel display.","children":["It is not just eye movements but **the actual execution of arm movements** that can be influenced by **syntactic ambiguity** and its interaction with the **visually defined context**."]},{"text":"With one apple, the hand moved from the apple toward the box, but with ambiguous instructions its path **curved toward the empty towel**, most strongly in the middle of the movement.","children":["With two apples, the ambiguous and unambiguous paths were nearly identical."]},{"text":"The **Euclidean distance**, the straight-line distance between the ambiguous and unambiguous paths at each moment, grew larger with one apple, and with two apples it peaked lower and earlier.","children":["A competing interpretation bends the path of the hand even when the movement ends at the correct place."]}],"sentence-17":[],"sentence-18":[{"text":"**The** is a **determiner**, a word that opens a **noun phrase**, so once the parser meets “the” it expects a noun soon, possibly with an adjective or another modifier first.","children":["“The” names nothing by itself, but it limits which kinds of words can come next."]},{"text":"The expectation is about a **grammatical category** and not about one particular word, because many nouns could follow, such as pizza, dog or book, or an adjective could come first, as in “the hot pizza.”","children":["Words that fit the expectation are processed smoothly, while words that break it cause difficulty."]}],"sentence-19":[{"text":"**Pizza** is a **noun** that completes the noun phrase opened by “the,” and it is the **head of the noun phrase**, the central word the rest of the phrase is built around.","children":["“The pizza” can now serve as the subject, so the parser expects a verb or verb phrase saying what the pizza did or what happened to it."]},{"text":"Recognizing the word gives two kinds of information. Its **category**, noun, tells the parser how it can combine with other words, and its **meaning**, a kind of food, sets up expectations about plausible events such as being eaten or baked.","children":["Both grammatical and meaning-based expectations guide how the next words are processed."]}],"sentence-20":[{"text":"**Was** is a past-tense form of **be**, and it can act as an **auxiliary**, a helping verb that joins another verb to mark grammatical information such as tense or voice.","children":["In “the pizza was eaten,” was helps form a **passive** with the participle eaten, and in “the pizza was in the oven,” it links the subject to a location phrase."]},{"text":"After “was,” the sentence could describe where the pizza was, what it was like or what was done to it, so several structures stay open.","children":["The parser must keep the developing structure available while more words arrive, because the role of was depends partly on what comes next."]}],"sentence-21":[{"text":"**In** is a **preposition**, a word that expresses a relationship such as location or containment, and it begins a **prepositional phrase**.","children":["“In” does not name the location by itself, so it sets up the expectation of a noun phrase that supplies it, as in “in the oven” or “in the box.”"]},{"text":"After “the pizza was,” the word “in” narrows the possible structures, and the sentence is now heading toward a statement about where the pizza was.","children":["The expectation is grammatical, so the next word must be able to start a noun phrase, as “the,” “an” or a noun can."]}],"sentence-22":[{"text":"**The** begins a new **noun phrase** inside “in the …,” the phrase that will name the location.","children":["“The” does not supply the location by itself, so a noun is still needed, possibly with modifiers before it, as in “in the hot oven.”"]},{"text":"The parser now strongly expects a **noun**, and this expectation concerns a **word category** and not only a sensible topic.","children":["Knowing the sentence is about pizza makes words like oven or box likely, but the grammatical requirement holds whatever the meaning, so any word that cannot be part of a noun phrase will clash with it."]}],"sentence-23":[{"text":"**Eaten** is the **past participle** of **eat**, a verb form and not a noun, so it cannot complete the noun phrase that “in the” has set up.","children":["**“In the eaten”** puts a verb form exactly where a noun is required, which is a **word-category violation**, a word of the wrong grammatical category in a position that demands a different one."]},{"text":"A category error differs from a strange but grammatical meaning, because “The pizza was in the ocean” is odd in meaning but grammatically fine, while no unusual situation can rescue “the pizza was in the eaten.”","children":["Brain responses to **category errors** and **meaning errors** can be compared to see whether grammar and meaning are processed differently."]}],"sentence-24":[{"text":"The **early left anterior negativity**, or **ELAN**, is an **ERP** response elicited by **word class violations**. An ERP, or **event-related potential**, is electrical brain activity recorded at the scalp, averaged over many trials and timed to an event such as the onset of a word.","children":["Classic stimuli are **“The pizza was in the eaten”** and **“The scientist criticized Max’s of proof the theorem,”** where a participle or the preposition of appears where a noun is expected."]},{"text":"The ELAN is **early**, arising about a tenth to a fifth of a second after the critical word. It is also **left and anterior**, meaning strongest over the left front of the scalp, which raises the question of whether it is specific to language.","children":["It appears **specifically for word category violations**, such as a noun when a verb is expected, and its speed was taken as evidence for a very quick syntactic check of word categories before meaning is processed."]}],"sentence-25":[{"text":"With visually presented words, **the ELAN came from visual cortex**, the region at the back of the brain that processes what we see, as **Dikker, Rabagliati, Farmer and Pylkkänen (2010)** found.","children":["For nouns ending in a recognizable noun suffix, **“The beautifully princess …”** drew a larger early visual response than **“The beautiful princess …,”** because the adverb beautifully predicts a verb or adjective and princess comes as a surprise."]},{"text":"When a verb is expected, for example, **that prediction can feed back down to the earliest parts of the visual system** and predict visual patterns consistent with **the way verbs are written**.","children":["The very early response to the error might reflect **a mismatch between that predictive process and what finally appears on the screen**."]},{"text":"A word is **morphologically marked** as a noun or verb when a part of it, such as a suffix, signals the category. Later comparisons answer whether the effect needs such marking, because it also appears for nouns with a typical noun form but not for neutral nouns.","children":["The other possibility raised is a split between **open-class** content words and **closed-class** function words."]}],"sentence-26":[{"text":"Many English words can be either a **noun** or a **verb**, and the rest of the sentence decides which.","children":["**Perches** is a noun in “the bird perches seem easy to install” and a verb in “the bird perches comfortably in the cage.”","**Needs** is a noun in “the student needs were not being met” and a verb in “the student needs to be more focused.”"]},{"text":"The word form alone does not settle its grammatical role, so the **continuation** after the ambiguous word resolves it.","children":[]},{"text":"Words also differ in **phonological form typicality**, which is how closely a word’s sound pattern resembles typical nouns or typical verbs.","children":["Across several thousand words, nouns generally sound more like other nouns and verbs more like other verbs, but there are also **verb-like nouns** and **noun-like verbs** that sound like one category while belonging to the other."]}],"sentence-27":[{"text":"In **form typicality**, **“perches” is noun-like** and **“needs” is verb-like**, which are learned statistical tendencies in how words sound, though both words can still fill either role.","children":[]},{"text":"For **perches**, the noun use in “the bird perches seem easy to install” matches its form, and the verb use in “the bird perches comfortably in the cage” goes against it.","children":["For **needs**, the verb use in “the student needs to be more focused” matches its form, and the noun use in “the student needs were not being met” goes against it."]},{"text":"Each word appears once as a noun and once as a verb, so the same form is compared in a **matching** and a **mismatching** use.","children":["If form typicality guides parsing, the mismatching uses should be harder to process, even though every sentence is grammatical."]}],"sentence-28":[{"text":"Reading is **slower when a word’s required category conflicts with its form typicality**.","children":["The measure is the extra reading time at **disambiguation**, the point where the continuation makes the category clear, compared with the **point of ambiguity**, the ambiguous word itself, adjusted for word length."]},{"text":"For **noun-like homonyms** such as perches, resolving the word as a noun added only a small cost, while resolving it as a verb added a much larger one.","children":["For **verb-like homonyms** such as needs, the pattern reversed, because a noun resolution added a large cost and a verb resolution added almost nothing."]},{"text":"The ambiguous word stays the same in each pair and only the continuation changes, so the extra cost comes from a continuation that demands the category the form does not match.","children":["Parsing uses learned statistics about how word forms sound, along with the structure the sentence finally requires."]}],"sentence-29":[{"text":"The **ELAN effect for nouns where a verb is expected** is limited to cases where **syntactic information**, meaning morphemes that indicate noun-ness, or **statistical form-based information**, meaning “nouniness,” is **physically available** in the word.","children":["A **morpheme** is the smallest meaningful part of a word, such as a noun suffix, and “nouniness” is how much a word’s form resembles typical nouns."]},{"text":"The early visual response peaks about a tenth of a second after a word appears. It was reliably larger for the unexpected version with **bimorphemic nouns**, as in “The beautifully princess …,” and with **typical nouns**, as in “The tastelessly soda …,” than for the expected version.","children":["**No effect was observed for neutral nouns like “infant” that lack strong cues**, since “The cute infant …” and “The cutely infant …” did not differ."]},{"text":"An abstract category mismatch alone is not enough, because the early response appears only when the arriving word carries physical evidence against the predicted form.","children":[]}],"sentence-30":[{"text":"**Sentence processing fits in four sentences**, and **“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously”** stands for the idea that **syntax is independent of semantics to some extent**, since a sentence can be grammatically correct but meaningless.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The horse raced past the barn fell”** stands for the idea that syntactic structure is often highly **ambiguous**, or really **vague**, though we notice only where things fail.","children":["Reading times for preferred and unpreferred structures suggest that syntactic processes **take shortcuts to save memory** and do not activate every possible parse."]},{"text":"**“Put the apple on the towel in the box”** stands for the idea that **context, pragmatics and other factors interact with syntactic processing from very early on**.","children":["**“The bird perches were comfortable”** stands for the idea that the very earliest syntactic effects in the brain are driven in part by **form typicality**, not necessarily by morphosyntactic structures, and seem related to **predictions about the physical forms of words**."]}],"sentence-31":[],"aphasias-01":[],"aphasias-02":[{"text":"**Aphasia** is a loss or impairment of language caused by brain damage, and in the eighteen seventies German neurologists such as **Lichtheim** and **Wernicke** began observing it more systematically.","children":["They ascribed specific losses of function to **“disconnections” between “centers,”** where a **center** is a brain region that stores one kind of language representation and a disconnection is damage to the pathway between two centers."]},{"text":"In **Lichtheim's “House” model**, **A** is the center for **sound images**, **M** is the center for **movement images**, which are motor plans for saying words, and **B** is the center for **concepts**.","children":["A lost ability to **understand spoken words** might come from a break between **A and B**, because the sound of the word arrives but cannot reach its concept.","A lost ability to do **verbatim repetition** might come from a break between **A and M**, because the heard word cannot pass directly to the plan for saying it."]},{"text":"This kind of **“box and arrow” model** is foundational in **aphasiology**, the study of aphasia, and in modeling typical language function, as in the **interactive activation model**.","children":["This history comes from **Eling and Whitaker (2009)**."]}],"aphasias-03":[{"text":"**Norman Geschwind** revived the box-and-arrow approach in the nineteen seventies, and the result is the **“Geschwind-Wernicke model”**, which ties language functions to brain regions and the pathways that join them.","children":["**Broca's area** sits in the left frontal lobe, **Wernicke's area** sits farther back in the temporal lobe, and the **arcuate fasciculus** is the bundle of nerve fibers that connects them.","The **central sulcus**, or fissure of Rolando, separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe, and the **lateral sulcus**, or fissure of Sylvius, separates the temporal lobe from the lobes above it."]},{"text":"In the century between Wernicke and Geschwind, linguistics developed a model in which **syntax was independent** of other aspects of language and had **its own computational principles**, so researchers could theorize about dissociations in more sophisticated ways.","children":["In a **dissociation** one ability is impaired while another is spared, and in a **double dissociation** one patient loses the first ability and keeps the second while another patient has the reverse pattern.","A double dissociation is strong evidence that the two abilities depend on separate systems."]}],"aphasias-04":[{"text":"**Fromkin (1997)** describes the classic profiles of the two main aphasias, which are Broca's aphasia and Wernicke's aphasia.","children":[]},{"text":"In **Broca's aphasia**, speech has **word-finding pauses**, **loss of grammatical morphemes** and often **disturbed word order**, while comprehension of everyday conversation seems generally good even though **controlled testing reveals considerable impairments**.","children":["A **grammatical morpheme** is a small word or word part that marks a grammatical relationship, such as “the,” “is” or the past-tense ending “-ed.”","**Agrammatism**, meaning difficulty producing or understanding grammatical structure, is almost a synonym for Broca's aphasia, yet some patients with lesions in Broca's area are **not agrammatic** and some agrammatic patients would not be classified neurologically as Broca's aphasics."]},{"text":"In **Wernicke's aphasia**, speech is **fluent** with good intonation and pronunciation, but it has many **word substitutions**, **neologisms**, which are invented words, and **phonological errors**, which are errors in the sounds of words.","children":["These patients also have **comprehension difficulties**, so fluency is separate from whether the words carry a coherent message."]}],"aphasias-05":[{"text":"An attractive **dichotomy**, meaning a two-way division, describes **Wernicke's aphasia** as **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**, while **syntax stays roughly intact**.","children":["A patient reported by **Kriendler and colleagues (1971)**, asked about his health, gave a long smooth answer that began “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds” and never formed a clear message.","Another patient said “My wires don’t hire right” about his poor vision, and an aphasic physician used the neologisms **demaploze** and **tubaboys** when he was asked whether he was a doctor."]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** is described as **“telegraphic” speech**, with difficulty with **word order and grammatical particles** and relative **sparing of important content words**.","children":["A patient reported by **Badecker and Caramazza (1985)**, asked what brought him to the hospital, produced content words such as Dad, hospital and doctors with many pauses and without the grammatical words that would connect them."]},{"text":"The two patterns look like mirror images, because Wernicke's patients have fluent grammar with wrong words and Broca's patients have meaningful words with little grammar.","children":[]}],"aphasias-06":[{"text":"Stated in terms of what is damaged, **Wernicke's aphasia** reflects problems with **activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon**, and **Broca's aphasia** reflects **problems with grammar**.","children":["The **lexicon** is the mental store of words, **activation** makes a candidate word available, and **inhibition** suppresses competing words, so weak inhibition could let wrong words slip into speech."]},{"text":"That account is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**, because his patient **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan,”** could only produce the one jargon syllable “tan” while he seemed to understand what was being said.","children":["His other senses were intact, so the loss was specific to speech, which makes it a dissociation, and a modern examination would describe it at phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic levels."]},{"text":"So **Broca's actual patients would not necessarily be classified as Broca's aphasics** today, because they were arguably unable to produce speech at all due to **motor control issues**.","children":["They would be classified as having **apraxia of speech**, a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements needed to speak, which can limit output severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge."]}],"aphasias-07":[{"text":"The dichotomy is interesting partly because it lines up with a sharp distinction that late twentieth-century formal linguistics drew between **the grammar and the lexicon**.","children":["The **grammar** is the system of rules for combining words into sentences, and the **lexicon** is the store of individual words, which must be memorized."]},{"text":"The distinction starts with **Chomsky's formulation of the computational problem of language learning**. A new string is recognized as a sentence because it is **generated by the grammar** each person has internalized, and not because it matches some familiar item.","children":[]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** also argued for the **independence of syntax and semantics** with “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” and “furiously sleep ideas green colorless,” because neither has occurred in an English speaker's experience and yet **only the first is grammatical**.","children":["Both are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless, so the difference must come from grammar as its own system.","The aphasia dichotomy maps onto this split, with Wernicke's aphasia as a disorder of the lexicon and Broca's aphasia as a disorder of the grammar."]}],"aphasias-08":[{"text":"The dichotomy also fits the **speech production model**, where the **functional level** arouses word meanings and **semantic roles**, which are the parts participants play in an event, and the **positional level** gets word forms in grammatically appropriate order.","children":["The conceptual **message level** comes before these two, and **phonological encoding** and the **speech gesture level** come after them."]},{"text":"At the functional level, **lemmas have an arbitrary relationship to their referents**, which means nothing about a concept determines its word, while at the positional level **syntactic trees and affixes are the product of abstract syntactic processes**.","children":["A **lemma** is a word's entry in the lexicon, linking its meaning to its grammatical properties, and an **affix** is a word part added to a stem, such as “-ly.”"]},{"text":"Words like **HOUSEBOAT** and **DOGHOUSE** seem to be an objection, because their meanings are clearly not arbitrary, but **speech error evidence** places **derivational morphology**, which builds new words from existing parts, at the positional level.","children":["In the error **“easy enoughly,”** the speaker meant “easily enough,” and the “-ly” suffix moved to the wrong word as a separate piece, so a grammatical operation must have added it."]}],"aphasias-09":[{"text":"Popular theories of **sentence processing** hold that the **syntactic structure** of a sentence is **computed independently of meaning** as people listen or read, so an autonomous parser builds the structure first.","children":[]},{"text":"**Isolating these “parsing” mechanisms** requires carefully constructed stimulus materials, such as “The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite,” which has two structures built from the same words.","children":["The **preferred** structure attaches “with the dynamite” to the **verb phrase**, so the dynamite is the instrument, and the **unpreferred** structure attaches it to the **noun phrase** “the safe,” so the phrase tells which safe."]},{"text":"One possibility is that **Broca's patients generally understand what is being said even though they have a core grammatical deficit**, because in conversation word meanings, familiar events and context all point toward the same message.","children":["A real test of a parsing deficit needs sentences whose grammatical relationships cannot be guessed from the most likely event, such as reversible sentences where either person could plausibly do the action."]}],"aphasias-10":[{"text":"None of this theory was available to **Broca** in the eighteen sixties, so for him it was interesting enough to **dissociate “articulate speech” from comprehension**.","children":["Leborgne could produce only one syllable but appeared to understand what was said to him, so speaking and understanding came apart."]},{"text":"The modern goal is to find patients whose difficulty is **more generally with syntax**, and the way to do it is to **test comprehension**.","children":["A **syntactic impairment** concerns the organization of grammatical relationships, so it should affect both producing and understanding sentences.","A comprehension test lets the patient respond without speaking, so if the patient still struggles with grammatical relationships, the deficit goes beyond articulation."]}],"aphasias-11":[{"text":"Showing that Broca's patients have **comprehension deficits** was interesting because **Broca's aphasia is almost always accompanied by motor dysfunction**, so agrammatic speech might merely reflect an attempt to communicate despite motor limitations.","children":["**Hughlings Jackson** made the related point that we observe what the individual can do despite the impairment, so agrammatic speech does not literally mean that **the grammar itself is injured**.","A version of this idea from **Lenneberg (1973)** says that the strain of speaking is so great that the patient speaks asyntactically in order to economize effort."]},{"text":"**Chomsky's distinction between competence and performance** also applies, where **competence** is a person's underlying knowledge of language and **performance** is the actual use of that knowledge.","children":["When motor deficits obviously interfere with performance, **comprehension may provide a clearer window into competence**."]},{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** therefore tested comprehension, because normal understanding of grammar would mean a motor performance problem, while failure to understand grammatical relationships would mean the deficit reaches the grammar itself.","children":[]}],"aphasias-12":[{"text":"**“Man bites dog”** is the newspaper example of an unexpected event, and **psycholinguistic tests** are needed because ordinary conversation offers many **redundant cues**, which are separate sources of information that all point to the same meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"For “The dog bit the man,” a listener can combine DOG, BITE and MAN into the **most likely event**, with or without syntax, or rely on **canonical word order**, which is agent, then verb, then undergoer.","children":["The **agent** is the doer and the **undergoer** is the one the action happens to, and the **discourse context**, the surrounding conversation, clarifies a lot as well."]},{"text":"Broca's patients may depend on these **compensatory heuristics**, which are mental shortcuts that make up for a weakness elsewhere, so they should be tested with sentences like “The dog that the man is biting is angry.”","children":["In that sentence the **man** does the biting even though the dog comes first and dogs are more likely biters, so only grammatical analysis gives the right answer."]}],"aphasias-13":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** devised a list of exactly this kind of sentence, with four groups that change how much meaning helps the listener.","children":["**Control sentences** are simple and in canonical order, such as “The girl is kicking a green ball.”"]},{"text":"The other three groups are **center-embedded**, which means a relative clause sits in the middle of the main clause, so the first noun is acted upon and the doer is inside the clause.","children":["With **semantic constraints**, as in “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” meaning alone reveals who does what, because apples cannot eat boys.","In **reversible** sentences such as “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” either participant could do the action, and in **improbable** sentences such as “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” common sense points to the wrong answer."]},{"text":"A listener who relies on meaning should succeed on the control and semantically constrained sentences and fail on the reversible and improbable ones, while a listener who parses normally should succeed on all four.","children":[]}],"aphasias-14":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task**, in which people heard a sentence and chose which of the pictures matched it, so accuracy reveals who the listener thinks did what to whom.","children":["They compared healthy controls with Broca's, conduction and Wernicke's aphasia patients on the four sentence types."]},{"text":"**Healthy controls** were about equally good at all sentence types, while **Wernicke's patients were poor overall**, even on simple control sentences, probably because of their general comprehension deficits.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca's patients** did well when meaning supplied the answer but had particular difficulty keeping **“who did what to whom”** straight when **syntax conflicted with common sense**, as in the reversible and improbable sentences.","children":["This specific comprehension deficit was taken as evidence that **Broca's aphasia impacts syntax specifically**.","Their paper calls it a dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes, because Broca's patients lose the **algorithmic** process of grammatical analysis and keep the **heuristic** shortcuts based on meaning."]}],"aphasias-15":[{"text":"A more specific theory of the **“parts of language”** opens up new ways of exploring **structure-function relationships**, which are links between a brain structure and the function it supports.","children":[]},{"text":"Instead of only sorting aphasics by good or poor comprehension, or by more or less fluency, researchers can **isolate problems with particular aspects of language**.","children":["One patient might know word meanings but assign the wrong roles in complex sentences, while another might produce a fluent grammatical frame filled with words that miss the message."]},{"text":"The key division is **the grammar versus the lexicon**, where grammar concerns the rules that relate words in a sentence and the lexicon concerns stored words and retrieving the right ones.","children":["A **grammar deficit** predicts trouble with reversible sentences but not with word meanings, and a **lexical deficit** predicts trouble with word meanings and naming but not with grammatical structure."]}],"aphasias-16":[{"text":"There are **problems with this dichotomy**, and they start with a task in which **Dick and colleagues** had participants listen to **four types of sentences** and indicate **which animal was doing the action**.","children":["A dog and a cat were on a screen, and the event was always a dog biting a cat while the sentence structure changed."]},{"text":"Two types put the doer first, the **active** “The dog is biting the cat” and the **subject cleft** “It’s the dog that is biting the cat,” so choosing the first animal mentioned gives the right answer.","children":["A **cleft** sentence splits a simple sentence with “It’s” and “that” to put focus on one part."]},{"text":"Two types put the receiver first, the **object cleft** “It’s the cat that the dog is biting” and the **passive** “The cat is bitten by the dog,” so the first-animal shortcut fails.","children":["Only a listener who parses the grammar succeeds on all four types."]}],"aphasias-17":[{"text":"When aphasia patients and **healthy aging controls** do this task, a pattern emerges much like the one **Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** observed.","children":["Elderly controls are nearly perfect on all four sentence types, and **Wernicke's** patients score low on every type, near chance."]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia patients have particular difficulty with the syntactically complex stimuli**, which are the object clefts and passives, and these are **noncanonical** structures that depart from the usual doer-first order.","children":["Their performance is **overall not so great**, because even on simple active sentences they are far below the controls, so a general drop combines with an extra cost for complex structures."]},{"text":"The other patient groups also drop on complex sentences. **Anomic** patients, whose main problem is word finding, stay fairly accurate on actives and subject clefts and drop on object clefts and passives, and **conduction** aphasia patients fall sharply on object clefts.","children":[]}],"aphasias-18":[{"text":"The same task was given to **healthy undergraduates** under conditions that **stress the language system**, such as speeding up the speech, adding noise, or asking people to remember lists of numbers as a secondary task.","children":["**Compressed** speech is played faster than normal, a **low-pass filter** removes the higher frequencies so speech sounds muffled, and the digit lists use up memory resources."]},{"text":"Accuracy stayed high on actives and subject clefts, but **object cleft and passive sentences were particularly vulnerable to all the manipulations**, and the drop grew larger when two stressors were combined.","children":["With muffled speech that was also sped up, accuracy on object clefts and passives fell to about two thirds."]},{"text":"These students have no brain damage, so stressing the processing system is enough to produce an aphasia-like profile, which suggests that the comprehension deficits in Broca's aphasia may be a **“performance” issue after all**.","children":["Complex structures may demand more processing resources, so patients may keep their grammatical knowledge but lack the resources to use it efficiently."]}],"aphasias-19":[{"text":"**Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked whether the comprehension deficits of Broca's aphasics are **language-specific** by having patients **match an action to an object**.","children":["In the **non-linguistic** version a drawing has a boy pantomiming an action such as licking, and patients choose the matching object from pictures such as an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster.","In the **linguistic** version the action was given in words instead of by pantomime."]},{"text":"Controls were nearly perfect in both versions, while aphasic patients made more errors in both and somewhat more in the linguistic version, so **Broca's aphasics had difficulty whether the stimuli were linguistic or not**.","children":["Patients made the most errors when the wrong choice, called the **distractor**, was **semantic**, meaning related in meaning to the correct object, and fewer with unrelated or **affordance-based** distractors, where an **affordance** is an action an object allows."]},{"text":"A purely grammatical deficit predicts good performance on the nonverbal version, so the difficulty there points to a broader problem with action meanings that is shared with language.","children":[]}],"aphasias-20":[{"text":"Performance in the **non-verbal version** of the action-matching task from **Saygin and colleagues (2004)** was **significantly correlated with damage to Broca's area**.","children":["Damage linked to deficits in **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation** clustered in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, which includes Broca's area."]},{"text":"The task only asked patients to interpret a pantomimed action and choose an object, so they did not have to produce a sentence or work out the grammar of a spoken instruction.","children":[]},{"text":"Damage to Broca's area therefore affects **more than syntax**, because patients with damage there can struggle to connect an action with its meaning even when no language is involved.","children":["Any account of Broca's area has to explain both the grammatical comprehension findings and this nonlinguistic difficulty with actions."]}],"aphasias-21":[{"text":"**Saygin and colleagues** asked whether the comprehension deficits of Wernicke's aphasics are **language-specific** by having patients **match environmental sounds to pictures**.","children":["In the **nonverbal** version a speaker plays a sound such as a cow mooing, and patients choose between pictures such as a cow and a sheep.","In the **verbal** version the speaker plays a spoken phrase such as “cow mooing,” and patients choose between pictures such as a cow and a violin."]},{"text":"Controls, patients with **right hemisphere damage** and anomic patients were all nearly perfect, Broca's patients were a little less accurate, and **Wernicke's patients were particularly bad at this task**.","children":[]},{"text":"**No significant difference** was observed between the verbal and nonverbal versions, so the deficit is not specific to words.","children":["Their difficulty concerns linking sounds of any kind to their meanings, whether the meaning arrives through language or through ordinary sounds."]}],"aphasias-22":[{"text":"Deficits in **both verbal and nonverbal sound-picture matching** are associated with **Wernicke's area**.","children":["Eight patients were impaired in the nonverbal version and ten in the verbal version, and in both groups the damage overlapped most in the **posterior temporal area of the left hemisphere**, around Wernicke's area."]},{"text":"The verbal and nonverbal impairments therefore come from damage to the same general region, even though a spoken phrase carries meaning through words and an animal call carries it without language.","children":[]},{"text":"Both tasks require connecting meaningful sounds to what they represent, so Wernicke's area appears to support **meaningful sound processing in general**, with language as one important case.","children":[]}],"aphasias-23":[{"text":"How language-specific the deficits in aphasia are is **still a controversy**.","children":["Some patients are especially vulnerable to complex grammatical structures, yet the same patients can struggle with action meanings or environmental sounds in tasks with no language, so an account of aphasia has to cover both findings."]},{"text":"What is clear is **how the terms of the debate have evolved**, because the field now has relatively **sophisticated tests** that tap specific aspects of language, **quantitative analyses of behavior**, and **quantitative analyses of structure-function relationships**.","children":["The tests include reversible, improbable and cleft sentences and nonverbal matching tasks, and the structure-function analyses relate patterns of brain damage to performance across many patients."]},{"text":"The field has come **a long way from the nineteenth century**, when Wernicke and Lichtheim drew boxes and arrows from a small number of individual cases.","children":[]}],"aphasias-24":[],"aphasias-25":[{"text":"**Voxel-based lesion-symptom mapping**, from **Bates, Wilson, Saygin, Dick, Sereno, Knight and Dronkers**, formalizes **structure-function relationships** in aphasia with MRI data by relating the location of brain damage to the severity of symptoms across many patients.","children":["A **voxel** is a small three-dimensional unit of an MRI brain image, and a voxel is linked to a symptom when patients with damage there score much worse than patients without damage there."]},{"text":"The symptoms are **defined very broadly**, with **fluency deficits** linked most strongly to damage toward the front of the left hemisphere and **comprehension deficits** linked to damage farther back, in the posterior temporal region.","children":[]},{"text":"Data from a large number of patients is therefore consistent with a **rough double dissociation**, where **posterior regions** are associated with **perception** and **anterior regions** are associated with **production**.","children":[]}],"development1-01":[],"development1-02":[{"text":"**Nativist positions** grew out of **Chomsky's critique of Skinner**, the behaviorist who explained language with general learning mechanisms such as reward and punishment.","children":["For Chomsky the important feature of language is its **generativity**, the ability to produce and understand unlimited new sentences, so acquiring a language means **identifying the abstract grammar** that produces its sentences."]},{"text":"General learning mechanisms were **not up to this task**, because grammatical and ungrammatical sentences are not \"similar\" in any straightforward way, and because acquisition does not look like **operant conditioning**.","children":["Children are not reliably rewarded for grammatical sentences or punished for ungrammatical ones, so the reward and punishment contingencies do not hold."]},{"text":"Nativist approaches instead assert **innate knowledge of what language is like**, called **Universal Grammar**, plus **language-specific acquisition processes**, which are learning mechanisms built specifically for language.","children":["Chomsky argued that because all normal children acquire comparable grammars of great complexity with remarkable rapidity, human beings seem specially designed to do this."]}],"development1-03":[{"text":"**Categorical perception** is the pattern in which listeners hear differences between speech sounds in terms of categories, and early theories treated it as **language-specific** and **species-specific**.","children":["Only humans were known to show it, and only for speech sounds, so it looked like a unique feature of human language."]},{"text":"On a synthetic consonant continuum that changes in equal steps, listeners switch sharply in **labeling** from **/b/** to **/d/** and later to **/g/**, and **discrimination peaks near those boundaries** and drops inside each category.","children":[]},{"text":"It is a compelling example of how **similarity between stimuli is defined in linguistic, rather than physical, terms**.","children":["Two sounds that differ by the same acoustic amount sound almost identical inside one category and clearly different across a category boundary."]}],"development1-04":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** is measured through behaviors that change when an infant notices something new, so neither method asks the infant to name or produce a sound.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab, an infant learns to turn toward a sound when it changes, because a correct head turn earns an interesting visual reward such as a lit-up moving toy.","children":["Once the link is learned, a head turn after a sound change means the infant **heard the change**."]},{"text":"In **high-amplitude sucking**, the infant sucks on a pacifier attached to a pressure recorder, and strong sucks make a sound play.","children":["As the same sound repeats, the infant grows used to it, which is **habituation**, and sucking slows.","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has noticed the change."]}],"development1-05":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** tested categorical perception in **four-month-old infants** with **high-amplitude sucking**, using syllables that varied in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**, the delay between the release of a stop consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration.","children":[]},{"text":"Infants heard one syllable until sucking slowed, and then one of **three conditions** followed.","children":["In one, the sound changed by a fixed amount of VOT and **crossed an adult phonemic category boundary**, and in another it changed by the same amount but stayed in the **same category**.","In the **control** condition the same sound continued throughout."]},{"text":"Sucking **jumped back up** only after the change that crossed the boundary, while it showed little recovery after the within-category change and kept declining in the control.","children":["The two changes were matched in acoustic size, so infants were responding to **crossing the category boundary**, and the control establishes what happens with repetition alone."]}],"development1-06":[{"text":"The result from **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** was a big deal because categorical perception was considered the **first step in turning speech into a symbolic format** for later linguistic processes to use.","children":["A symbolic format stores a sound as a category, such as /b/."]},{"text":"**Four-month-olds are really little**, with gross motor milestones such as manipulating objects with two hands and holding the head and chest up while prone.","children":["Their color vision and general visual acuity are still \"in progress,\" and they are just starting to produce vowel-like sounds instead of creaky whines."]},{"text":"This is **very, very early in development** for an apparently language-specific ability to emerge, so it **seemed like evidence for innateness**.","children":["Speech categorization ran far ahead of other abilities, so it looked built in instead of learned through long experience."]}],"development1-07":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing** is the decline in sensitivity to speech contrasts that the language a child hears does not use.","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested **English-learning infants** on a **Hindi contrast** between two \"ta\"-like sounds and a **Salish contrast** between \"ki\" and \"qi\", counting how many infants at each age reached a discrimination criterion.","children":["At around six months most infants discriminated both contrasts they had never heard, fewer did a couple of months later, and only a small minority did by the end of the first year.","Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants discriminated their own native contrast nearly every time, so the decline **depends on language experience**."]},{"text":"Narrowing is related to categorical perception because infants are forming native categories, and sounds that their language groups together become harder to tell apart.","children":["A nonnative distinction that falls inside one native category comes to be heard as a **within-category difference**."]}],"development1-08":[{"text":"In a **very compelling narrative**, speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities** that are a **special property of the language system** and are present very early in development.","children":[]},{"text":"Children start off as **\"citizens of the world,\"** able to hear **all contrasts**, which means they have **innate, language-general knowledge** about speech that applies to the sounds of every language.","children":["They then **rapidly tune their perceptual systems** to the specific language in their environment."]},{"text":"The **role of learning** is then to **select** among the many innately specified phonetic contrasts and narrow the **inventory**, the full set of contrasts the child can perceive, to those meaningful in the native language.","children":["Learning works by elimination, because it drops the contrasts the native language does not use."]}],"development1-09":[{"text":"The narrative has **some problems**, and three concern **categorical perception** itself.","children":["It is **not limited to language**, because it also occurs for nonspeech sounds, and it is **not limited to humans**, because animals without language perceive speech sounds categorically.","It is also **task-dependent**, so results look more or less categorical depending on whether listeners name a sound or judge whether two sounds differ."]},{"text":"**Infant speech perception** is also **not actually \"universal,\"** because infants do not begin able to hear every contrast equally well.","children":["Some contrasts are hard for young infants to detect even when their own language uses them."]},{"text":"A full account of development must therefore explain contrasts that **improve with experience** as well as contrasts that are lost, so learning must build categories as well as remove them.","children":[]}],"development1-10":[{"text":"**Cutting and Rosner (1974)** tested categorical perception with **non-speech sounds** that varied in **rise time**, which is how quickly a sound grows to full loudness after it starts.","children":["A **short rise time** sounds like a **plucked** string and a **long rise time** sounds like a **bowed** string, and the stimuli included sawtooth waves, speech syllables and sine waves."]},{"text":"For sawtooth waves, listeners called short rise times pluck and long rise times bow, with a fairly sharp switch in the middle of the range.","children":["Discrimination was only a little above chance for most pairs, and it **peaked** for the pairs right where the labels switched."]},{"text":"A label boundary with better discrimination at that boundary therefore appears for sounds that have **nothing to do with phonemes**, so categorical perception can arise in **general auditory processing** without a mechanism specific to language.","children":[]}],"development1-11":[{"text":"**Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** used high-amplitude sucking to test whether **infants** show categorical perception of **non-speech sounds**.","children":["Infants heard one sound until sucking slowed, and then there was **no change**, a change **within categories** or a change **between categories**."]},{"text":"Sucking stayed low after no change and after the within-category change, and it **jumped back up** only after the between-category change, before declining again as the new sound became familiar.","children":[]},{"text":"Infants responded to a non-speech change that **crossed a category boundary**, which is the same pattern that **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** found for speech.","children":["Adults label plucked and bowed sounds categorically and infants discriminate nonspeech changes categorically, and neither needs language knowledge, so early categorical perception reflects a **general auditory ability**."]}],"development1-12":[{"text":"**Ohlemiller and colleagues (1999)**, following **Kuhl and Miller (1975)**, found categorical perception of **speech sounds** in **chinchillas**.","children":["The animals were trained with **operant conditioning** that either the zero-lag stimulus or the eighty-millisecond-lag stimulus was an **SD**, a **discriminative stimulus** signaling that a response will be rewarded with food.","The lag is the **voice onset time**, and humans hear the zero lag as /ga/ and the eighty-millisecond lag as /ka/."]},{"text":"Tested on intermediate VOTs along the continuum from /ga/ to /ka/, chinchillas labeled short VOTs /ga/ almost every time and long VOTs almost never, with the same **steep, categorical switch** as human listeners.","children":[]},{"text":"**Increasing the intensity of the burst and aspiration noise** shifted the boundary to a **shorter VOT**, made the switch steeper, or both, for one chinchilla and one human listener.","children":["The **burst** is the brief noise at the release of a stop, and **aspiration** is the breathy noise that follows a voiceless release.","Animals without language sort a speech continuum into categories, so categorical perception of speech is **shared with other species**."]}],"development1-13":[{"text":"**Narayan and colleagues (2010)** found that **some contrasts are less salient**, meaning less easy to notice, than others for infants.","children":["They tested **nasal consonants** in the **onset** of a syllable that differ in **place of articulation**, with /m/ made at the lips, /n/ just behind the teeth and /ŋ/ at the soft palate."]},{"text":"Infants heard a syllable that either repeated or switched, and longer looking after a switch meant they noticed it.","children":["English-hearing infants of four to five months looked longer for the native switch between **[ma] and [na]** but not for the nonnative switch between **[na] and [ŋa]**."]},{"text":"The place contrast between /na/ and /ŋa/ is **not readily perceived by four-month-olds, independent of language experience**, while /ma/ and /na/ is **relatively salient**.","children":["The gap comes from the sounds themselves, so not every contrast begins equally available."]}],"development1-14":[{"text":"**Narayan and colleagues (2010)** found that between six and ten months, exposure to the contrast between /na/ and /ŋa/ in a **Filipino language environment increases its salience**.","children":["Filipino treats [na] and [ŋa] as different syllables, so Filipino-learning infants hear the contrast regularly.","The younger Filipino-learning infants did not yet look longer when the syllable switched, while those near the end of the first year did."]},{"text":"So it **cannot be that all speech contrasts are available innately**, because sensitivity to this contrast starts out weak even for infants whose language uses it.","children":[]},{"text":"**Exposure to contrasts in the environment is necessary for acquisition in some cases**, so learning can strengthen a contrast as well as preserve it.","children":["Development therefore includes building up sensitivity to hard contrasts, not only losing sensitivity to unused ones."]}],"development1-15":[{"text":"The alternative to the nativist approach is the **emergentist approach**, which assumes that **associative learning mechanisms** are sensitive to **statistical regularities in the environment**.","children":["**Associative learning** is learning that two things go together because they repeatedly occur together, and statistical regularities are patterns in how often things occur and which occur together."]},{"text":"Classical conditioning is the simplest case, described with a **bell** neuron, a **food detector** neuron and a **salivation** neuron.","children":["Food already produces salivation, and when the bell repeatedly occurs with food, the **synapse** from the bell neuron to the salivation neuron is **strengthened** until the bell alone produces salivation."]},{"text":"This can extend beyond conditioning to **pattern recognition**, which is identifying a recurring structure such as a speech sound across varied examples, because the same strengthening could link acoustic features that tend to occur together.","children":["Repeated co-occurrence in speech could then build categories that were never specified in advance."]}],"development1-16":[{"text":"When **Kuhl and others** talk about **learning the statistics of distributions in the environment**, they mean patterns like those of the **American vowels**, where a **distribution** describes how often each value occurs across many examples.","children":[]},{"text":"Each vowel occupies a region of a **vowel space** determined by the **first and second formants**, and the regions **overlap** heavily.","children":["A single pair of formant values can belong to more than one vowel, so listeners must learn where each vowel is centered and how far it spreads."]},{"text":"Maybe we learn the vowels of our language by **extracting the statistics of these distributions**, so a **\"prototypical\" /a/** is just the **mean**, or perhaps the **mode**, of all the /a/ sounds we hear.","children":["The mean is the average value and the mode is the most frequent value, and either way the prototype comes from experience."]}],"development1-17":[{"text":"**Maye and colleagues (2002)** tested whether infants really learn from distributions by having them **listen passively** to a continuum from [da] to [ta] that varied in **voice onset time**.","children":["In the **bimodal distribution**, sounds near each end were most frequent, which gives **evidence for two categories**.","In the **unimodal distribution**, sounds in the middle were most frequent, which gives **evidence for a single category**."]},{"text":"At test, infants heard stimuli three and six in **alternating** trials, where the two switched back and forth, or **non-alternating** trials, where one repeated.","children":[]},{"text":"**Only infants pre-exposed to the bimodal distribution** were sensitive to the difference between the two kinds of trials.","children":["The unimodal infants looked about equally long on both, as if they had learned to treat stimuli three and six as **members of the same category**.","Brief exposure to a distribution changed how infants grouped the sounds, which supports statistical learning of speech categories."]}],"development1-18":[{"text":"Experiments give evidence that **social interaction is important for learning**.","children":[]},{"text":"Nine-month-old American infants were exposed to **Mandarin** either live, with a speaker playing and reading in person, or through audio or television, while controls heard English.","children":["On a later head-turn test of a Mandarin phonetic contrast, only the **live** group beat the controls, so the same exposure is **more effective in person and interactive** than as a video."]},{"text":"In a second experiment, mothers of eight-month-olds responded either right after the infant vocalized, the **contingent** condition, or on a schedule copied from another infant, the **yoked-control** condition.","children":["Contingent feedback **increased the quantity and complexity of vocalizations**, and the infants stayed higher even after the responses stopped.","Contingent social interaction therefore seems to work like a **reinforcing stimulus**, either because humans find social interaction **innately reinforcing** or because we are innately good at using **social cues** to learn from other people."]}],"development1-19":[{"text":"**Statistical learning with constraints** is learning from patterns in the input that is guided and limited, and \"constraints\" covers a lot of things.","children":[]},{"text":"One constraint is on **which stimulus properties can be contrastive**, meaning able to distinguish words.","children":["Speaker identity and speech rate change the sound a great deal without changing the word, so they are not contrastive and learning must ignore them."]},{"text":"Other constraints are **based on earlier learning**, because once native categories are in place, new distinctions that cut across them become harder to learn, which is central to **sensitive periods**.","children":["**Social and interactive contexts** are a third constraint, because sounds are learned better in live, responsive interaction than from a recording."]}],"development2-01":[],"development2-02":[{"text":"Speech is **messy** because **there are no physical boundaries between words**, so **word segmentation**, the job of working out where one word stops and the next starts, is hard.","children":["In “I hope it’s in my desk,” the sound energy of each word flows straight into the next word with no silent gap."]},{"text":"The **pauses** that do occur come from **closures**, moments when the vocal tract is fully blocked as for /p/, /t/, /d/ and /k/, and closures are **as likely within words as between words**.","children":["A listener who treated every silence as a boundary would split some words and merge others."]},{"text":"Adults are still **really good at finding words** in this “sea of sounds” because they can lean on words they already know.","children":["**Learning to do this** is the harder problem, because infants must discover the words in the stream before they can learn what the words mean."]}],"development2-03":[{"text":"Several **cues to word boundaries** exist, but none works for every word. Some words **appear in isolation**, as in “Hello” or “Stop,” but not all words do.","children":["**Phonotactics**, the sound sequences a language allows, helps because **some sequences of phonemes are very unlikely within words**, such as /db/ in “wordboundary” and /tʃk/ in “speechcode.” The /vm/ in “movement” is a caution, because movement is one word."]},{"text":"**Stress patterns** help because **most English words are stressed on the first syllable**. **Jusczyk and colleagues (1999)** familiarized infants about seven and a half months old with one word and then played them passages.","children":["Infants familiarized with “kingdom,” stressed on its first syllable, **preferred the kingdom passage**, while infants familiarized with “device,” stressed on its second syllable, **showed no such preference**."]},{"text":"**Stress may signal the beginnings of words** in connected speech, so an infant who starts a word at each stressed syllable finds kingdom but cuts device wrongly.","children":["This cue fails for some English words and in other languages, because languages put stress in different places."]}],"development2-04":[{"text":"**Transitional probabilities** are another cue to word boundaries, and learners pick them up through **statistical learning**, which means learning the patterns in how often things occur together.","children":[]},{"text":"In “pretty baby,” the syllable **/ti/** is **very strongly predicted by the syllable /prɪ/**, because pretty is a common word and “ty” very often follows “pre.”","children":["This is **less true for syllable pairs across words**, because many different words can follow “ty,” so “ba” is poorly predicted."]},{"text":"The frequency of “pretty” divided by the frequency of “pre” is **far greater** than the frequency of “tyba” divided by the frequency of “ty.”","children":["Syllables inside a word predict each other strongly and syllables that cross a word boundary predict each other weakly, so a learner who counts which syllable follows which can find the joints in speech."]}],"development2-05":[{"text":"Stated abstractly, **the likelihood of syllable B following syllable A is greater within words than between words**, and that likelihood is the **transitional probability** from A to B.","children":["Inside a word the same syllables always come in the same order, while almost any word can come next at a boundary, so a drop in transitional probability is a good guess for a boundary."]},{"text":"The cue is **probabilistic**, so it works most of the time but not every time.","children":["“What’s that in the road a/head?” can be heard as “a head” or “ahead” with identical sounds, so the meaning of the whole sentence decides the boundary.","“I scream you scream we all scream for ice cream” works because “I scream” and “ice cream” have almost the same sounds."]},{"text":"Transitional probabilities work alongside stress, phonotactics and words heard alone, and listeners fall back on **context** when the cues clash or are missing.","children":[]}],"development2-06":[{"text":"**Saffran and colleagues (1996)** separated **statistical information from other cues** to find out whether **transitional probabilities** alone can be used to identify words, so the stimuli were tightly controlled.","children":[]},{"text":"The speech was **monotone**, with no changes in pitch, duration or amplitude, so stress could not mark word beginnings.","children":["Every syllable was a **simple CV syllable**, one consonant followed by one vowel, so there were **no phonotactic cues**.","The syllables were **presented continuously** with **no pauses**, so silence could not signal a boundary."]},{"text":"The researchers built an **artificial language** of **three-syllable “words”** such as tokibu, gikoba and gopila, and joined them in random order into one long stream.","children":["The only clue to where each word began and ended was the transitional probability between syllables, so infants who find the words must be using that cue alone."]}],"development2-07":[{"text":"The stream of the artificial language has **no breaks at all**, as in “tokibugikobagopilatipolutokibu,” and this unbroken run of syllables is exactly what the infant hears.","children":["Four words, tokibu, gikoba, gopila and tipolu, are **hidden in the stream**, and each recurs many times in random order."]},{"text":"The syllables still follow **regular patterns**, because inside tokibu “to” is always followed by “ki” and “ki” is always followed by “bu.”","children":["After “bu” the stream may continue with “gi,” “go” or “ti,” depending on which word comes next."]},{"text":"Everything needed to find the words is in the stream, because a learner who **tracks which syllables follow which** discovers that some pairs are fully predictable and others are not.","children":[]}],"development2-08":[{"text":"Marking each of the four words in its own color makes **tokibu**, **gikoba**, **gopila** and **tipolu** visible to a reader, but the infant gets no such marking and hears one unbroken run of syllables.","children":[]},{"text":"Each word keeps the **same inner order** every time while its **neighbors keep changing**, so transitional probabilities are high inside each word and lower across word edges.","children":["The syllables “to,” “ki” and “bu” always come in that order, but tokibu may be followed by gikoba, gopila or tipolu."]},{"text":"The word edges are exactly where **transitional probability drops**, so a learner who notices where predictability drops recovers where one word ends and the next begins.","children":["The signal itself contains no spaces, so the boundaries come only from which syllables reliably belong together."]}],"development2-09":[{"text":"After a long stream of syllables, infants are trivially more familiar with sequences they have heard than with ones they have not, so a preference for heard sequences could reflect **familiarity** alone. The test added two details to remove that possibility.","children":["**Half of the words were repeated more frequently** than the other half.","The test compared words with **“part-words”** that were **matched for frequency** with real words, where a part-word is a three-syllable sequence joining the end of one word to the start of the next."]},{"text":"In “tokibu gikoba gopila gikoba tokibu gopila tokibu gikoba gikoba tokibu,” the word **GOPILA** and the part-word **BU-GIKO** each appear twice.","children":["BU occurs four times and is followed by GI only twice, so the probability of **GI given BU** is two out of four, while both transitional probabilities inside GOPILA are two out of two."]},{"text":"Frequency is equal, so any difference between words and part-words must come from **how predictable the syllables are**.","children":[]}],"development2-10":[{"text":"The test produced a **novelty effect**, because **infants looked longer in the direction of the “part-word” stimuli** than toward the words, and the difference was statistically significant.","children":[]},{"text":"Longer looking is a **novelty preference**, because infants tend to look longer at whatever seems new.","children":["The words had grown familiar during listening, so they were less interesting, while the part-words seemed less familiar although infants had heard them just as often."]},{"text":"The result suggests that infants **had learned the statistical regularities of the speech stream** and **were not merely picking up on simple frequency of occurrence**.","children":["Words and part-words were matched for frequency, so only their transitional probabilities could separate them, and infants used that predictability after a short exposure."]}],"development2-11":[{"text":"**Identifying word referents** is the problem that follows segmentation, where a **referent** is whatever a word refers to. Children who can find the words still have to learn what the words mean, and they do it with further evidence from the situation in which a word is used.","children":["A child may hear “cat” while looking at a cat, but recognizing that sound pattern and knowing that it names the furry animal are separate achievements."]},{"text":"Segmentation gives a recurring chunk of sound and **says nothing about meaning**, and any scene holds many objects, parts, properties and actions.","children":["Learning a word therefore means **linking its form to the speaker’s intended meaning**, so the learner needs evidence about which thing the speaker has in mind."]}],"development2-12":[{"text":"**The inductive problem of early word learning** is that a single experience fits many possible meanings, where **induction** means reasoning from particular examples to a general rule.","children":["A new word heard while watching a cat might mean **CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**, the name **FELIX**, a property such as **FURRY** or **ALIVE**, an action such as **WALKING**, or a part such as **WHISKERS** or **LEG**."]},{"text":"All the candidate meanings make up the **hypothesis space**, and every candidate fits what the child sees, so nothing in one scene singles out the intended meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"This is a version of **Quine’s “gavagai” problem**, in which a linguist hears a speaker of an unknown language say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by.","children":["The word might mean rabbit, undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else.","Children still learn words fast, because joint attention, intentional cues and default strategies narrow the possibilities."]}],"development2-13":[{"text":"Children acquire word meanings partly through **joint attention**, which is **sharing an attentional focus with another person**.","children":["Two people look at the same object, and each knows that the other is attending to it too."]},{"text":"**Gaze following**, which means looking where someone else is looking, **emerges around nine months of age**, so the infant follows an adult’s eyes to an object.","children":[]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**, because the infant grasps that the adult’s attention is aimed at something specific.","children":["If the adult then says a word, the shared object becomes the most likely referent, so joint attention shrinks the **hypothesis space** by tying the word to what the speaker is attending to."]}],"development2-14":[{"text":"**Baldwin and colleagues** found that **joint attention optimizes word learning**, using two groups of infants who heard a novel word while playing with an object.","children":["In the **joint attention group**, an **adult interacting with the baby** labeled the object the baby was playing with.","In the **no joint attention group**, a **voice from behind a screen** labeled the object the baby was playing with."]},{"text":"The groups matched in that the word came while the infant was focused on the same object, so they differed only in whether a visible person was engaged with the infant.","children":[]},{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds learned the novel word in the joint attention group but not in the no joint attention group**.","children":["Hearing a word while seeing an object was not enough, because infants needed evidence that a speaker meant the word to refer to that object."]}],"development2-15":[{"text":"Children narrow the **hypothesis space** for new words partly through **intentional cues from the speaker**, which are signals of what the speaker means to refer to, such as **eye gaze direction**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to sixteen-to-seventeen-month-olds and eighteen-to-nineteen-month-olds under two conditions.","children":["In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the **toy the infant was focused on** while saying the new word, so both cues pointed to one toy.","In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a **toy in a bucket**, a different toy from the infant’s, while saying the new word."]},{"text":"The discrepant condition separates the infant’s focus from the speaker’s focus, so it tests whether infants treat the **speaker’s gaze as evidence** of meaning.","children":["Infants who attach a word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy, while infants who follow gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket."]}],"development2-16":[{"text":"**Gaze direction** helps a child work out which object a speaker is naming when several objects are present, because a speaker who looks at one object while saying a new word ties the word to that object.","children":["The child can follow the speaker’s eyes to the **intended referent**."]},{"text":"A speaker’s focus can be **different from the child’s own focus**, so following it means using information about someone else’s attention.","children":["The child can then attach a new word to an object the child was not looking at when the word was spoken."]},{"text":"Gaze **narrows the possible meanings** although the sound of the word gives no clue, because the child understands that a speaker’s eyes point to what the speaker means.","children":["The result is a far more specific mapping than linking the word to everything in view."]}],"development2-17":[{"text":"Intentional cues also include **affective and behavioral cues**, where affective cues are the speaker’s emotional expressions and behavioral cues are the speaker’s actions.","children":["An expression can reveal whether an object is the one the speaker was looking for, and an action such as reaching can reveal what the speaker wants."]},{"text":"**Akhtar and colleagues** found that **infants used adults’ facial expression to identify the intended referent of a novel word**.","children":["The adult’s emotional reaction to each object signaled whether it was the one meant, so infants linked the new word to the object that the expression picked out."]},{"text":"Several objects may be present when a word is spoken, and the speaker’s reaction can **single out one of them**.","children":["Satisfaction signals that the speaker found the wanted object, and disappointment signals that an object is not the one meant."]}],"development2-18":[{"text":"A speaker’s **emotional reaction** works as an **intentional cue** that can pick out the intended object from several candidates.","children":["Satisfaction or excitement can tie one object to what the speaker was trying to find or do, so the learner can use that tie to interpret a new label."]},{"text":"**Intention is conveyed through more than gaze direction**, because facial expressions and actions also reveal what a speaker means.","children":["The helpful information lies in how the adult responds to each object, and connecting the label to that response shrinks the **hypothesis space**, the set of meanings the word might have."]},{"text":"Infants who use emotional reactions are **following the speaker’s goals**, so the word attaches to the object that meets the goal and objects that disappoint the speaker are ruled out.","children":[]}],"development2-19":[{"text":"Children also bring **word learning strategies**, which are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**, where a default is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","children":["These strategies **narrow the hypothesis space for new words**, so children have a place to start when other cues are weak."]},{"text":"The **whole object assumption** leads a child to expect a new word to name **a whole object**, not one of its parts, its color, its texture or an action.","children":["A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat will favor “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”"]},{"text":"The assumption **rules out many unlikely meanings at once** and fits the way adults usually name objects for children.","children":["It is only a starting preference, so children still learn words for parts and properties once the evidence points that way."]}],"development2-20":[{"text":"**Mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**, is the default assumption that **objects can have only one name**, and its implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**.","children":["It **emerges during the second half of the second year**."]},{"text":"Suppose a child knows the word “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view.","children":["The child takes a new word to mean the parrot because the dog already has a name, and the child makes this choice without any gaze or emotional cue."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity works with the **whole object assumption**, which says that a new word names an object, by adding that the object has no name yet.","children":["It is a default, so later learning can override it when an object turns out to have more than one name."]}],"development2-21":[{"text":"Under **mutual exclusivity**, a child who hears an unfamiliar word gives it to an object that **has no known name yet**, because an object the child can already name is an unlikely target for a new word.","children":["The choice comes from comparing the objects that are in view."]},{"text":"**Prior vocabulary** decides which objects count as already named, so two children in the same scene can guess differently if they know different words.","children":[]},{"text":"Vocabulary growth **builds on itself** for that reason, because a child who knows many names can rule out many objects.","children":["The unnamed objects that remain become the likeliest referents, so every word the child knows helps narrow the meaning of the next new word."]}],"development2-22":[{"text":"When children produce only **one, two or three words at a time**, it is hard to tell how they are putting words together, because very different processes can produce the same short utterance.","children":[]},{"text":"**Menn** distinguishes four kinds of early utterance.","children":["**“Just a bunch of relevant words”** fit the situation with no grammar linking them, and **formulas** are phrases memorized as whole units without the child knowing their parts.","**Word combinations** join separate words productively, and **stable constructions** are consistent patterns that are **not necessarily adult-like**."]},{"text":"A child might say “Up!” or “Pick you up!” or “Mommy up!” and the longer “Pick you up!” may be a formula copied whole while the shorter “Mommy up!” may be a true combination.","children":["Length cannot settle it, so **a full description of the context** of an utterance is **necessary but often not sufficient** to distinguish among these."]}],"development2-23":[{"text":"**Comprehension** runs ahead of **production**, because **even children at the one-word stage in production can use basic transitive word order in comprehension**.","children":["A **transitive** sentence has a verb with a subject and an object, and English puts the actor first and the one acted on after the verb."]},{"text":"The **structure-mapping account** of **early syntactic bootstrapping**, from **Gertner and Fisher**, describes how children use sentence structure to help learn meaning.","children":["From the input sentence “The boy is tickling the girl!” the child builds a **partial sentence representation** with tickle, boy as first noun and girl as second noun.","From the observed scene the child builds **conceptual representations** of possible events, including tickling with the boy as **agent**, the doer, and the girl as **patient**, the one affected."]},{"text":"A **one-to-one mapping between nouns and participant roles** lines up the two nouns with the two participants in the tickling event, so noun order tells the child who did what.","children":[]}],"development2-24":[{"text":"A **generalization test** asks whether children have really learned the **transitive structure**, because children who have learned it will **generalize it to new verbs**.","children":["Choosing the scene that fits “The duck is gorping the bunny” requires knowing that the first noun is the doer and the second is the one acted on, because “gorp” is a made-up verb."]},{"text":"**Gertner and colleagues (2006)** found that **two-year-olds can understand this**.","children":["Children saw two novel actions side by side, one with the duck acting on the bunny and one with the bunny acting on the duck, while hearing the sentence.","They **looked significantly longer at the duck acting on the rabbit** than at the reverse."]},{"text":"Children this age typically have a **mean length of utterance (MLU)**, the average number of morphemes in each utterance, of **around two**, so their own three-morpheme utterances might be like “Duck gorp bunny.”","children":["Their speech is short and incomplete, yet they understand the full transitive structure."]}],"l1-critical-01":[],"l1-critical-02":[{"text":"**Critical periods** and **sensitive or “optimal” periods** both describe how the timing of experience affects learning, and they differ in how strict the time limits are.","children":[]},{"text":"A **critical period** has a **discrete, “programmed” onset and offset**, and learning, which is change in response to the environment, is **strictly limited to this period**.","children":["There is **no sensitivity to the environment before or after** the window, so any late improvement counts against a strict critical period."]},{"text":"In a **sensitive period**, **learning is possible throughout the life span** but is **optimal during particular windows**, which may have a **gradual onset and offset** that experience itself can affect.","children":["Late learning is harder but still possible, and early learners keep a lasting advantage.","Even the classic critical period in vision fits the term sensitive period better, and the same question comes up for language."]}],"l1-critical-03":[{"text":"**Amblyopia** is **poor vision in one eye despite normal peripheral function**, which means the eye itself works and the trouble lies in how the brain handles that eye's input.","children":[]},{"text":"Three conditions can lead to amblyopia in one eye.","children":["**Strabismus** is a misalignment of the eyes, so the brain receives two conflicting images, and a **cataract** is a clouding of the lens that blocks or blurs the incoming light.","**Anisometropia** is a large difference in focusing power between the eyes, so one eye's image is much blurrier than the other's."]},{"text":"**Periods of deprivation from normal visual input** caused by these conditions can leave **poor vision long after the conditions have been remediated**, meaning corrected.","children":["Correcting the eye restores its input, but the brain may still fail to use that input well, so a temporary early problem can have a lasting effect."]}],"l1-critical-04":[{"text":"**Amblyopia occurs because of competitive dynamics between cortical inputs**, because both eyes send input to **visual cortex** and the two inputs **compete** for control of the neurons there.","children":["**Abnormal vision in one eye** can lead to **“colonization”** of that eye's cortical representation by the other eye, so the stronger eye takes over neurons that would have served the weaker one."]},{"text":"**Monocular deprivation**, which means blocking or degrading input to one eye, reveals the takeover in mice.","children":["Within a few days the **dendritic spines**, the small projections where neurons receive connections, become unstable, and then spines are lost and cut-off fibers break down in **Wallerian degeneration**.","After more than a week the open eye's axons sprout and new spines form in the space the deprived eye lost."]},{"text":"When normal function returns to the deprived eye, it **stays at a competitive disadvantage**, so **normal experience cannot restore normal cortical responses**.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-05":[{"text":"**Treatments for amblyopia are based on competition**, and one of the most effective is **intermittently patching the “good” eye**.","children":["The stronger eye is patched because covering it blocks its input for part of the day, so the brain has to rely on the weaker eye."]},{"text":"Patching **restores the competitive balance between the eyes** and lets the brain's own **plasticity**, its ability to change its connections in response to experience, **restore normal function**.","children":["The patch does not repair the eye, because it only changes the experience the brain receives while the weaker eye wins back cortical connections."]},{"text":"The treatment is **most effective early in development** and **may be ineffective by late childhood**, because the visual system becomes less plastic as it matures.","children":["The timing of treatment matters as much as the treatment itself."]}],"l1-critical-06":[{"text":"**Levi and Polat (1996)** found that **plasticity is not abolished entirely, even well into adulthood**, in patients with amblyopia who were as old as their fifties.","children":[]},{"text":"The adults did **long-term, intensive training on specific psychophysical tasks** with the affected eye, and they **improved in both task performance and general visual function**.","children":["A **psychophysical task** measures the relationship between a physical stimulus and what a person perceives, such as the smallest contrast a person can detect.","Over many thousands of trials the **threshold**, the smallest stimulus a person can reliably detect, **dropped steadily**, then **leveled off** for a long stretch, and then **dropped again** to a new lower level."]},{"text":"A lower threshold means better vision, so the adult visual system keeps some **experience-dependent plasticity** after ordinary late treatment stops working.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-07":[{"text":"**Li, Ngo, Nguyen, Lam and Levi (2008)** found that **playing video games enhances visual acuity and visual attention in adult amblyopia**. The adults played an action video game, a first-person shooting game set in a battle scene, with the amblyopic eye.","children":["**Visual acuity** is the sharpness of vision, and **visual attention** is the ability to select and focus on the relevant parts of a scene."]},{"text":"Action games demand fast visual judgments and constant attention, so they push the visual system to use the weak eye's input.","children":["The players' childhood visual development was long over, so the improvement came from the training itself."]},{"text":"The result adds to the evidence for **plasticity** in adult amblyopia, because an early optimal window can close without removing every possibility of improvement.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-08":[{"text":"**Amblyopia is a classic example of a sensitive period**. A **peripheral disturbance in early experience** can lead to **life-long disruption in visual function**, and that long-term loss is the **sequela**, the lasting result, of a **temporary deficit during experience-dependent organization of the brain**.","children":["The brain organized itself around abnormal input, and that organization stays after the eye is corrected.","Treatment is **most effective during a limited developmental window**."]},{"text":"However, new evidence suggests that **the right kind of experience can reverse deficits late into adulthood**, as intensive perceptual training did in **Levi and Polat (1996)** and video game play did in **Li, Ngo, Nguyen, Lam and Levi (2008)**.","children":["A strict critical period would rule out any later improvement, so a strong early window with some later capacity for change fits a **sensitive period** better."]}],"l1-critical-09":[{"text":"The vision evidence is related to language through two lines of work.","children":[]},{"text":"The first is the early attempt by **Lenneberg (1967)** to synthesize **developmental neurology**, the study of how the nervous system develops, with the notion of a **“language acquisition device”**, a proposed innate capacity for learning language.","children":["Lenneberg asked whether changes in the developing brain could open and close a window for language, so that learning depends on the state of the brain as well as on the input."]},{"text":"The second is **studies of early deprivation**, with data from **neglected children** and from **Deaf children with limited input to sign**.","children":["These people first met an accessible language unusually late, so comparing them with typical learners tests whether the age of first exposure affects the final outcome."]}],"l1-critical-10":[{"text":"In **Biological Foundations of Language**, **Lenneberg (1967)** was attempting to synthesize **contemporary linguistics** with what was known about **brain development**.","children":[]},{"text":"Studies of language development were starting to find a fairly robust, **stage-like pattern**, which held across Austrian, British and American children.","children":["**Single words** come first, from late in the first year into the second year, then **two-word phrases**, and then **sentences of five words or more** toward the end of the third year.","Language milestones line up with motor milestones, so reduplicated babbling becomes frequent when infants stand holding on, and words such as **mama** or **dada** emerge when they walk with one hand held."]},{"text":"The idea that language has **maturational milestones** was seen as consistent with the **unfolding of a biological program**, because Lenneberg proposed that brain maturation sets the pace for both language and motor development.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-11":[{"text":"**Data on typical brain development were sparse** in Lenneberg's time, because they came almost entirely from **post-mortem analyses of tissue**, meaning tissue studied after death.","children":["Measurements of **neuron size** had no data from age two to age eighteen, a gap that overlaps much of the period of language acquisition."]},{"text":"Nonetheless, **Lenneberg (1967)** observed that **abrupt changes in neuron size and packing density before age two** might be **permissive for the beginning of language acquisition**.","children":["**Packing density** is how closely neurons are crowded together, and **permissive** means the changes make language acquisition possible without directly causing it."]},{"text":"On this view, **the critical period seems to open after this initial period of rapid development**, once the early burst of brain growth settles.","children":["Lenneberg filled the gap in the data with a reasonable guess that later research would need to test."]}],"l1-critical-12":[{"text":"Studies of recovery from stroke gave more extensive data, and they found that **aphasia is surprisingly rare in children with brain lesions**, which are areas of damaged brain tissue.","children":[]},{"text":"In extreme cases a **hemispherectomy** was performed, which removes or disconnects a whole cerebral hemisphere, typically to relieve seizures in people with epilepsy.","children":["**Children in whom the left hemisphere is removed nonetheless seem to develop typical language abilities**, while **adults do not recover language after this procedure**.","In the summary of cases that **Lenneberg (1967)** based on **Basser (1962)**, nearly all children who lost the left hemisphere before the teens kept or improved their speech, while every adult who lost it had permanent aphasia."]},{"text":"These outcomes led Lenneberg to hypothesize that a key factor in **closing the critical period** is the development of adult-like **cerebral dominance**, the specialization of one hemisphere, usually the left, for language.","children":["In a young child language is not yet fixed in the left hemisphere, so the right hemisphere can take over."]}],"l1-critical-13":[{"text":"Systematic studies of children with **perinatal strokes**, which happen from the prenatal period through the first four weeks after birth, make the findings on recovery more complicated.","children":["Outcomes are **clearly much better than for adults with similar injuries**, but the injury still leaves effects."]},{"text":"**Early language deficits appear in both right-hemisphere and left-hemisphere patients**, and **some deficits are worse for children with right-hemisphere damage**.","children":["That is the opposite of the adult pattern, because adult language problems mainly follow left-hemisphere damage."]},{"text":"**Language outcomes are generally in the low-normal range**, which suggests a **persistent delay rather than a catching up**.","children":["Early plasticity is real but limited, because a young brain can reorganize to support language while the reorganized system may not work as well as the original would have."]}],"l1-critical-14":[{"text":"**Curtiss and colleagues (1974)** studied the **effects of early deprivation** through the case of **Genie**, who was thirteen when researchers first met her and so thin that she looked six or seven.","children":[]},{"text":"From the age of twenty months she was confined to a small room under increasing physical restraint, harnessed to an infant's potty chair or kept in a sleeping bag inside a covered crib.","children":["She was **physically punished by her father if she made any sounds**, and her father and older brother never spoke to her, though they sometimes barked at her like dogs.","The home had no television or radio, and she had almost no contact with other people."]},{"text":"When she was hospitalized for malnutrition, Genie could not stand up straight or chew food, and she **did not speak, cry or produce any vocal sounds**.","children":["Her lack of language came with severe physical, social and emotional deprivation, so the effect of missing language input cannot be separated from her other hardships."]}],"l1-critical-15":[{"text":"During the **four years** that her language was observed, Genie **learned quite a bit**.","children":["She came to **comprehend hundreds of words**, **some aspects of morphology** and **some prepositions**, and she was **particularly good with plurals**.","**Morphology** is the system of meaningful word parts, such as the plural ending."]},{"text":"A test of prepositions had her move **buttons and plastic boxes** according to spoken instructions such as “Put the red box behind the blue box.”","children":["She gave **more correct than incorrect responses for every preposition** tested, and she was strongest on **in** and **on**."]},{"text":"Some language can therefore be **acquired late**, because this learning took place after puberty, long after the window Lenneberg proposed.","children":["The learning was uneven across different parts of language."]}],"l1-critical-16":[{"text":"Genie's **production lagged severely behind her comprehension**.","children":["**Some lag is typical of development**, because all children understand more than they can say, and Genie had also been **punished for making sounds** as a child."]},{"text":"Her **grammatical abilities developed much more slowly than her vocabulary**, as a test of **conjunctions** with **five familiar objects in a row** made clear.","children":["She was asked to point in response to items such as “Show me the fork and pencil,” “Show me the crayon or the knife” and “Show me either the spoon or the crayon.”","**And** requires pointing to every named object, while **or** requires pointing to just one, which depends on understanding a logical relationship."]},{"text":"At first she treated **or** like **and** and pointed to both objects, and later she told them apart but never understood **either/or**.","children":["She answered almost every **and** sentence correctly and got most **or** sentences wrong, so her grammar stayed weak despite a vocabulary of hundreds of words."]}],"l1-critical-17":[{"text":"Genie's case leaves **lots of questions**, so one case cannot separate the possible causes of her difficulty.","children":["Genie may have been **learning disabled** from birth, and **neglect and abuse** may have left residual impacts on her social development that affected learning beyond the missing language input.","**Funding to study her was pulled after four years**, so nobody can know what she might have learned after a longer time."]},{"text":"Nonetheless, the **disparity between her vocabulary development and her grammatical development** is widely interpreted as suggestive that **grammar learning in a first language is specifically impaired after a sensitive period**.","children":["She learned hundreds of words but made little progress with grammar, so on this interpretation vocabulary can be learned at any age while grammar depends on early exposure."]}],"l1-critical-18":[{"text":"**Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002)** studied deaf people who acquired a first language late. **Many deaf children do not get sign language input** but have an **otherwise typical upbringing**, so unlike Genie their only unusual experience is the lack of early accessible language.","children":[]},{"text":"Children aged nine to fifteen **have difficulty learning American Sign Language (ASL) if it is their first language**, while a **comparison group** who went deaf after initially learning a spoken language **do much better**.","children":["**These differences persist into adulthood**, and adults with no early language recalled complex ASL sentences about half as well as adults with an early spoken language.","In judging whether complex English sentences were grammatical, deaf adults with no early language were close to chance, while deaf adults with ASL in infancy matched hearing adults with an early spoken language."]},{"text":"The advantage comes from **having any language early**, signed or spoken, because early language provides the foundation for learning languages later.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-19":[{"text":"The evidence supports age effects in learning a first language, or **L1**, but it does not settle whether the window is a **“critical period”** or a **“sensitive period”**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Recovery from stroke, and certainly hemispherectomy, looks very different in childhood and adulthood**, because children can develop language after damage that would leave adults permanently aphasic.","children":["**Deprivation of input does not appear to extend the critical period**, because Genie and the deaf adults in **Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002)** who learned sign late both have lasting difficulty, especially with grammar.","The window seems to follow a maturational schedule whether or not language has been experienced."]},{"text":"All of these data come from **highly unusual cases with lots of other things going on**, so firm conclusions are difficult to reach.","children":["There is also a **lack of animal models**, and **gene knockout** experiments, which disable specific genes to test their role, cannot be done with people."]}],"l1-critical-20":[],"l1-critical-21":[],"l2-learning-01":[],"l2-learning-02":[{"text":"**Babies are overrated** as language learners, so talk of **sensitive periods** for a second language, or **L2**, should not be carried too far.","children":["A typical toddler at a year and a half uses at least twenty words, and an adult who knew only about that many after the same stretch of language instruction would count as a very poor learner."]},{"text":"**Adults initially learn more quickly than children**, largely because of **transfer from L1**, the first language, and because of **better general cognitive skills** such as memory, attention and reasoning.","children":[]},{"text":"Where adults do worse is **ultimate attainment**, which is the final level of skill a learner reaches, because adults **rarely become native-like**.","children":["This may also be due in part to transfer, since the first language that gives a fast start can also hold a learner back from native-like skill."]}],"l2-learning-03":[{"text":"Some toddler **language milestones** are really **general conceptual abilities**, such as dealing with **spatial relations between objects** or with **event representations**, which are mental representations of who does what in an event.","children":["Understanding “in and out” and “off and on” depends on spatial relations, and pretend play such as giving teddy a drink depends on event representations."]},{"text":"**Later learners** of a second language already have much of this in place, so they can focus on how those concepts are represented in the **target language**, meaning the language being learned.","children":["A toddler has to build the concepts and the words at the same time, which is a bigger job."]},{"text":"Later learners also already know another language, and that knowledge affects how the second language is learned because some of its ready-made ways of expressing concepts fit the new language and others clash with it.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-04":[{"text":"**Transfer** is the use of native language, or **L1**, processes and representations for a second language, or **L2**, so a learner brings L1 habits for hearing sounds, building sentences and finding words into the L2.","children":[]},{"text":"Transfer can have both positive and negative impacts on second-language learning and performance.","children":["**Positive transfer** happens when an L1 pattern matches what the L2 requires, so the learner gets a head start.","**Negative transfer** happens when an L1 pattern conflicts with the L2, so errors appear wherever the two languages differ."]},{"text":"A man who asks “Do you not be happy with me as the translator of the books of you?” is using English words with the structure of another language, because word-by-word translation carries over the word order of the first language, which is negative transfer in grammar.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-05":[{"text":"Transfer affects **phonetic and phonological learning**, the learning of speech sounds, because **categorical perception** leads to **acquired similarity**, which means that sounds a language groups into one category come to sound more alike.","children":[]},{"text":"Hindi has **minimal pairs**, which are pairs of words that differ in only one sound. Each pair contrasts a **dental** consonant, made with the tongue against the teeth, with a **retroflex** consonant, made with the tongue curled back toward the roof of the mouth.","children":["Two words that both sound like “tap” to an English speaker mean hot and a kind of beat, two that sound like “dab” mean pressure and a kind of fruit, and two that sound like “ari” mean saw and crooked."]},{"text":"Native English speakers tend to use their **alveolar** category, made with the tongue on the ridge behind the teeth, to interpret both dental and retroflex consonants, so the two Hindi sounds seem the same.","children":["In this case transfer makes learning the L2 contrast **more difficult**, because the learner must separate sounds that English taught them to treat as one."]}],"l2-learning-06":[{"text":"Transfer also affects **grammar learning**, and **classifiers** are a somewhat unusual grammatical feature across languages. A classifier is a word that must come between a number or demonstrative and a noun, and the choice depends on the kind of thing the noun names.","children":["In Chinese **yí gè rén**, “one person,” **gè** is the general classifier, while **bǎ** goes with objects that have a handle, **běn** with books, **zhāng** with flat objects and **zhī** with long, thin objects."]},{"text":"Native English speakers learning Chinese **have difficulty** with classifiers, because saying “a chair,” “that dog,” “the car” or “one book” in English never depends much on noun semantics, while in Chinese different nouns require different classifiers.","children":["One way to think about this is that English speakers **learned to ignore the cues for classifiers** as part of learning English grammar, so learning Chinese means attending again to cues such as shape and function."]}],"l2-learning-07":[{"text":"**Grammatical gender** is another place where transfer causes problems, and it is different from social or cultural gender because it sorts nouns into classes, often called masculine and feminine.","children":["There is nothing “male” about a book or “female” about liberty, yet French **le livre** is masculine and **la liberté** is feminine."]},{"text":"Grammatical gender matters for **agreement**, which means that pronouns, adjectives and some forms of verbs all need to be coordinated with the gender of the noun.","children":["English nouns have no grammatical gender, so English speakers have no habit of tracking it."]},{"text":"French is famous for **synonyms with different gender**, such as **auto** and **voiture** for car, **rivière** and **fleuve** for river, and **vélo** and **bicyclette** for bicycle, so gender has to be learned word by word.","children":["Word endings give clues, since masculine nouns often end in -age or -isme, as in le fromage, and feminine nouns often end in -ie or -ion, as in la boulangerie and la nation."]}],"l2-learning-08":[{"text":"**Transfer is not all bad**, because vocabulary learning in some L1 and L2 situations is aided by the **similarity of words across languages**.","children":["French **auto**, **rivière** and **bicyclette** resemble English auto, river and bicycle, and Japanese **コロッケ (korokke)** and **モノレール (monoreru)** mean croquette and monorail."]},{"text":"Sometimes the similarity is due to **shared etymology**, meaning a shared word history, and words with a common origin are called **cognates**, as with rivière and river.","children":[]},{"text":"Sometimes the similarity is due to more recent **borrowing**, which produces a **loan word**, a word that one language takes from another.","children":["Korokke and monoreru were borrowed from European languages with their sounds adapted to Japanese.","Both kinds of word give **positive transfer**, because the learner already knows a similar form with a similar meaning and can anchor the new word to it."]}],"l2-learning-09":[{"text":"**Not all similar-sounding words are cognates**, and the **bias** to interpret L2 words as related to similar-sounding L1 words can be very strong.","children":["A **false friend** is a word that looks or sounds like a word in another language but means something different."]},{"text":"Spanish “Arnold está embarazado” does not mean that Arnold is embarrassed, because **embarazado** means **pregnant**, so an English speaker relying on the sound gets an embarrassing result.","children":[]},{"text":"The link to the familiar L1 word forms before the learner checks the meaning, so the same tendency speeds learning when the meanings match, as with cognates, and **leads to errors** when they do not.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-10":[{"text":"**De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** asked in the lab whether people use **cognates** in “second language” learning. They used **paired-associate learning** of made-up words, in which people study pairs and later recall one member of a pair when given the other.","children":["Dutch speakers were simply presented with Dutch words and their non-word partners and asked to learn the pairs, so Dutch stood in for the first language and the made-up words for a new second language."]},{"text":"The made-up words varied in their **similarity to the Dutch associate** and in **concreteness**, where a concrete word names something that can be seen or touched and an abstract word names an idea.","children":["The four kinds of pair were **gitaar** with **gettar** (“guitar,” concrete, cognate), **tijger** with **duunze** (“tiger,” concrete, non-cognate), **domein** with **domaan** (“domain,” abstract, cognate) and **herstel** with **groop** (“repair,” abstract, non-cognate)."]},{"text":"Participants were then tested a week later on **retention**, meaning how well they remembered the pairs after a delay without study.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-11":[{"text":"In the study by **De Groot and Keijzer (2000)**, **cognates were learned more rapidly and also remembered more easily**. Recall was tested across three training sessions and again in a fourth session after a week with no training.","children":["Recall rose across training for every kind of word, but concrete cognates started far higher than concrete non-cognates, and abstract non-cognates started lowest of all.","After the week without training, concrete cognates kept most of what was learned while abstract non-cognates fell sharply, and responses were faster for cognates in every session."]},{"text":"The cognate advantage was largest in the first session, so **cognate status can contribute to the very earliest stages of word learning** in a second language.","children":["Concreteness also had a large effect that adds to the cognate effect, and one explanation for it is called **context availability**, the idea that concrete words are easier to link with a supporting context."]}],"l2-learning-12":[{"text":"The experiment by **De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** is a good one because it tested more than **receptive vocabulary**, which here means how easily participants could produce the Dutch word given its made-up associate.","children":["A cognate-like made-up word already resembles the Dutch word, so a participant could answer by noticing the resemblance without having learned the pair, and that result alone would not be very impressive.","The meanings of **gitaar**, **tijger** and **domein** could be guessed without any Dutch training, and made-up cognates such as **gettar**, **riffer** and **vialo** are only slightly changed Dutch words."]},{"text":"The study also tested **expressive vocabulary**, which the authors call **productive testing**, where the learner is given the Dutch word and must produce the made-up word.","children":["The learner has to recall the exact new form, so a cognate advantage on productive testing reflects **real learning of new forms**."]}],"l2-learning-13":[{"text":"Participants in the study by **De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** really learned the new forms, because the cognate advantage held in **productive testing** as well as in **receptive testing**. Both were measured right after learning and again at retest a week later, and the important measure is how much recall dropped over the week.","children":["In productive testing, where learners had to produce the new word, cognates lost about half as much as non-cognates, and concrete words were forgotten less than abstract words.","In receptive testing, where learners gave the Dutch word, scores were higher overall and cognates lost only a little while non-cognates again lost much more."]},{"text":"Cognates and concrete words were **forgotten less in both directions**, so their advantage extends from initial learning to long-term retention.","children":["Similarity to a known word helped learners form a **lasting memory** of the new word."]}],"l2-learning-14":[{"text":"A central research question is whether **similar L1 words are always activated when processing L2**, and there have been many studies of it.","children":["If first-language words that sound similar become active even when a bilingual is trying to use only the second language, then the two languages are never fully separate in the mind."]},{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** give one nice example with a **visual world experiment**, in which people hear spoken instructions while looking at a set of objects and their eye movements are recorded.","children":["Looks toward an object whose L1 name sounds like the spoken L2 word would reveal that the L1 word became active.","Eye movements capture that activation without asking for any report, while the person still goes on to choose the correct object."]}],"l2-learning-15":[{"text":"This kind of experiment was used earlier for **categorical perception** by **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)**, who studied gradient effects of within-category voice onset time (VOT) on **lexical access**.","children":["Participants saw a lamp, a ship, a bear and a pear and heard a word from a continuum running between “bear” and “pear,” which differ only in their first consonant."]},{"text":"**Eye movements** can tell us about **competition** between the possible responses that participants are considering.","children":["A person can click on the bear after looking briefly at the pear, and those looks mean that “pear” was active as a competitor."]},{"text":"The amount of competition between BEAR and PEAR seemed to depend on **distance from the category boundary**, with more looks to the competitor for sounds near the boundary between /b/ and /p/.","children":["The same method can ask whether a word in one language competes with a similar word in another."]}],"l2-learning-16":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found the **cohort effect**, where a **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds.","children":["Participants viewed a grid of pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions, with a **beaker** as the target, a **beetle** that shares its beginning, a **speaker** that rhymes with it and an unrelated **carriage**."]},{"text":"Looks to the beaker and the beetle **rose together early**, because both fit the first sounds of the word, and looks to the beetle fell once the sounds that distinguish the two words arrived.","children":["Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed smaller because its overlap comes at the end of the word, and looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"The cohort effect is this **early competition** from words that share a beginning with the target, so words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds and drop out when the input stops matching them.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-17":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** examined the cohort effect with **cross-language competitors**, which are objects whose name in the other language begins like the spoken target word.","children":[]},{"text":"They hypothesized that if **L1 words are always activated**, native Russian speakers should look at a book of matches when the target is “speaker” in English.","children":["English **speaker** begins with the same sounds as Russian **спички (spichki)**, which means matches, so the matches should draw looks if Russian is active during English listening.","The two words are tied only by **sound**, so looks to the matches during an English task directly measure activation of spichki."]},{"text":"Each English target came with an English competitor and a Russian competitor, as when **speaker** came with **spear** and **spichki**, or **marker** with **marbles** and **marka**, stamp.","children":["For Russian targets the roles were reversed, so Russian **spichki** came with English **spear** and Russian **spitsi**, knitting needles."]}],"l2-learning-18":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** first had Russian-English bilinguals follow instructions in English, which tests **competition during L2 spoken word recognition**.","children":[]},{"text":"When only one competitor was present, participants made eye movements to **within-language and between-language competitors to the same extent**, and far more often than to an unrelated control object.","children":["The Russian name of an object pulled the eyes as strongly as a similar English word did."]},{"text":"When both competitors were present, participants looked at the within-language competitor more, but they **still looked at the between-language competitor** about twice as often as in the control condition.","children":["The first language stays active during second-language listening, so bilinguals still choose the correct object while L1 words compete along the way."]}],"l2-learning-19":[{"text":"The same bilinguals in the study by **Marian and Spivey (2003)** then did the experiment in Russian, which tests **competition during L1 spoken word recognition**.","children":["A Russian within-language competitor still drew extra looks, so the cohort effect appeared in Russian."]},{"text":"**English words do not seem to be activated** when native Russian speakers do the task in Russian.","children":["An object with a similar English name drew only slightly more looks than the control object, and that small numerical difference is **not statistically significant**, which means it falls within ordinary chance variation."]},{"text":"The two experiments together give an **asymmetric** pattern, because Russian words became active during English tasks and English words did not become reliably active during Russian tasks.","children":["The asymmetry fits the idea that the first language is more firmly established and more easily activated than the second."]}],"l2-learning-20":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** explains interactions between L1 and L2 in terms of **spreading activation**, which means that activating one representation partly activates the representations linked to it.","children":["The model has an L1 lexicon, or vocabulary, an L2 lexicon and a store of concepts, with **lexical links** joining the two lexicons and **conceptual links** joining each lexicon to the concepts."]},{"text":"The **L1 lexicon** is assumed to be **larger** and **more strongly connected to relevant concepts**, so L2 words have weaker links to their meanings.","children":[]},{"text":"The **L2 lexicon** is assumed to have **strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon**, so activation can spread more readily from an L2 word to an L1 word than the other way.","children":["This occurs because this direction is emphasized in learning, since learners usually attach each new L2 word to its L1 translation."]}],"l2-learning-21":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** can explain what happens when the task of **Marian and Spivey (2003)** is done in L2 with the target “speaker.”","children":["The concept for “matches” **partially activates the Russian word for “matches,”** spichki, because L1 words are strongly connected to their concepts, and the sounds of English speaker also partly activate spichki because the two words begin alike.","This activity **feeds back to the conceptual activation** for “matches,” and that conceptual activation leads to **eye movements toward the L1 competitor**."]},{"text":"Speaker and spichki are linked only by sound overlap and share no meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"When the task is done in L1, the **conceptual links with L2** might be **too weak** to create this kind of interference reliably.","children":["An English competitor would need a strong link from the English word to its concept, so the unequal looks come from the unequal strength of the links."]}],"l2-learning-22":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** makes a **counter-intuitive prediction** even for **balanced bilinguals**, people who are about equally skilled in both languages.","children":["They do not look like “two monolinguals in one,” with each language running separately."]},{"text":"For a balanced bilingual the **conceptual links are similarly strong for both languages**, so each concept strongly activates its word in both languages.","children":["**Competitive dynamics**, in which words from the two languages keep competing for selection, and **inhibition**, the suppression of the word from the language not in use, will be engaged all the time."]},{"text":"This prediction has led to a lot of work on the advantages of bilinguals in non-linguistic **cognitive control** tasks, where cognitive control is the ability to manage attention and suppress irrelevant responses.","children":["Constant practice at selecting one language and suppressing the other might improve control of attention in general."]}],"l2-learning-23":[{"text":"So far second-language learning has followed one **sequence**, in which a person begins learning L1, later learns L2 and continues learning and using L1 the whole time.","children":[]},{"text":"The other sequence is one where the person **stops using L1** after L2 is introduced, so L1 is no longer heard or spoken and L2 takes its place.","children":["In that case L2 can appear to completely replace L1, although some residual knowledge may remain that later learning can unmask."]},{"text":"This situation separates two things that usually go together, which are **learning a language early** and **continuing to use it**.","children":["If early learning leaves a permanent foundation then traces of L1 should remain without use, and if continued use is needed then L1 might fade or disappear."]}],"l2-learning-24":[{"text":"**Pallier, Dehaene, Poline, LeBihan, Argenti, Dupoux and Mehler** used brain imaging of **language plasticity in adopted adults** to ask whether a second language can replace the first.","children":["They tested adults in their twenties and early thirties who had been adopted into French homes from Korean orphanages between about three and eight years of age.","These adults learned Korean as young children and stopped using it entirely after adoption, so they are a natural case of L1 use stopping."]},{"text":"The test was whether the adoptees had any **residual knowledge of Korean**, meaning any remaining trace of the earlier language.","children":["Monolingual French speakers with no Korean experience were the comparison group, so adoptees doing better on Korean-related tasks would mean that some Korean had survived."]}],"l2-learning-25":[{"text":"Korean has a **three-way stop contrast**, where a **stop** is a consonant made by fully blocking the airflow and then releasing it, such as /p/, /t/ or /k/.","children":["English and French have two kinds of stop at each place in the mouth, such as /b/ and /p/, while Korean has three, called **aspirated**, **plain** and **tense**.","An aspirated stop has a strong puff of air, a plain stop has a lighter release and a tense stop is made with a stiffened throat."]},{"text":"The contrast appears in the **bilabial** series **pʰul** “grass,” **pul** “fire” and **p’ul** “horn,” in the **dental** series **tʰal** “mask,” **tal** “moon” and **t’al** “daughter,” and in the **velar** series **kʰo** “nose,” **kok** “music” and **k’ok** “exactly.”","children":[]},{"text":"French speakers have no such contrast and tend to hear the three sounds as two, so adoptees who kept any Korean sound knowledge should hear the differences **better than French speakers**.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-26":[{"text":"**Ventureyra and colleagues** found that Korean adoptees in France were **no better than monolingual French speakers** at discriminating the Korean contrasts.","children":["Adoptees, French native speakers and Korean native speakers heard pairs of sounds and judged whether they were different."]},{"text":"The pairs contrasted plain with tense, plain with aspirated and tense with aspirated stops, along with a plain and tense contrast for s.","children":["Korean speakers heard these differences nearly every time, while adoptees and French speakers rarely heard them."]},{"text":"Two control conditions confirm that the adoptees **could do the task**, because all three groups heard a **vowel** contrast nearly every time and correctly called **identical** sounds the same almost every time.","children":["The difficulty was specific to the Korean stop contrasts, where early Korean experience left **no measurable advantage**."]}],"l2-learning-27":[{"text":"In a more extensive study, **Pallier and colleagues** found that adoptees were **no better than monolingual French speakers** on three tasks.","children":["When **distinguishing Korean from a collection of largely unrelated languages**, both groups rated Korean and Japanese sentences as similarly Korean-like and rated Polish, Swedish and Wolof lower.","When **identifying the correct Korean translation of French words**, both groups scored close to chance."]},{"text":"When **deciding whether a short excerpt was repeated from a recorded sentence they had just heard**, both groups did best on French and equally well on Korean, Japanese and Polish.","children":[]},{"text":"These are **not standard psycholinguistic tasks**, because Pallier and colleagues were looking for **any remaining trace of Korean** with tasks meant to catch even weak knowledge.","children":["They did not find any, so on every measure the adoptees looked like people who had never learned Korean."]}],"l2-learning-28":[{"text":"**Pallier and colleagues** also found that listening to French activated the **classical language network**, including **superior temporal** and **inferior frontal** regions, in both groups.","children":["The superior temporal region is the upper part of the temporal lobe and is involved in processing speech sounds, and the inferior frontal region is the lower part of the frontal lobe and includes Broca's area."]},{"text":"Activity for Polish, Japanese and Korean was subtracted from activity for French, and French always produced more language-network activity than the foreign language in both the French group and the Korean adoptees.","children":[]},{"text":"**Subtracting Korean from French** might have revealed a difference between groups if any **residual processing ability for Korean** were left, because Korean would then activate the language network of the adoptees more.","children":["It did not, so the brains of the adoptees treated Korean as a foreign language, which agrees with the behavioral tests."]}],"l2-learning-29":[{"text":"Interactions between L2 and L1 can also be thought of in terms of **competitive dynamics**. Imagine that **L1 is represented as a set of connections** within and between different brain regions, which are built and maintained through use.","children":[]},{"text":"If those connections are never activated again, they could be **“taken over” by L2**.","children":["L2 is used constantly, so its connections strengthen and spread, while unused L1 connections weaken and can be claimed by L2."]},{"text":"The same process occurs in vision with **monocular deprivation** in mice.","children":["The connections of the deprived eye first show increased spine motility, then **spine loss and Wallerian degeneration**, the breakdown of cut-off nerve fibers.","Later the fibers of the other eye show **axon sprouting and new spine birth**, so the eye that keeps receiving input takes over the lost space."]}],"l2-learning-30":[{"text":"The evidence from the adoptees says that a second language **apparently can replace the first**, because they showed no behavioral or brain sign of their childhood Korean.","children":[]},{"text":"What remains open is whether **traces of L1** are left behind that can be **“salvaged,”** meaning recovered or put to use.","children":["Such a trace might not appear on a test of current ability and could still make **relearning** easier."]},{"text":"Testing current recognition asks what a person can do with the language now, while testing later learning asks whether new learning benefits from something left over.","children":["Relearning is therefore the measure that could reveal a trace that the adoptee tests could not detect."]}],"l2-learning-31":[{"text":"**Au, Knightly, Jun and Oh**, from the group of Terry Au, studied undergraduates who **overheard Spanish frequently as children** but never learned to speak it.","children":["All participants started classroom Spanish in their early teens and were tested at the end of a second-year college Spanish course."]},{"text":"On **pronunciation** these childhood overhearers were far ahead of typical late L2 learners and closer to native speakers.","children":["Their **voice onset time** for voiceless stops matched native speakers almost exactly, while late learners produced much longer, English-like values.","They also showed more **lenition**, the softening of voiced stops between vowels, their accent ratings fell between the other two groups, and they knew some childhood slang."]},{"text":"On **grammar** they had little or no advantage, since gender agreement, number agreement and grammaticality judgments were about like those of late learners.","children":["Early listening left traces in the sound system that were **unmasked** by later study."]}],"l2-learning-32":[{"text":"Taking the data from the **Korean adoptees** together with the data from the **overhearers**, two conclusions follow.","children":["If L1 is removed from the environment entirely, **L2 can appear to completely replace it**, as the adoptees showed no Korean on any test of current ability.","This replacement is **not necessarily complete**, because there may be some **residual knowledge** that can be “unmasked” with later learning, as with the Spanish pronunciation of the overhearers."]},{"text":"The two populations also differ in potentially important ways.","children":["The adoptees were tested on current knowledge with no new Korean instruction, while the overhearers were tested after years of Spanish classes that gave hidden knowledge a chance to surface, so the results are hard to compare directly."]}],"l2-learning-33":[{"text":"Several **potentially important differences** between the adoptees and the overhearers might explain why only one group kept traces of an early language.","children":["**Korean is rarely heard in France** while Spanish is common in Los Angeles, and a **heritage language** spoken by family members differs from a language not spoken by anyone you know.","**Cognates** are common between Spanish and English and rare between Korean and French, and Spanish and English have a **shared writing system** while Korean uses a different script from French."]},{"text":"This is a **general issue** with work on second language acquisition and bilingualism, because languages and learning situations differ in many ways that are **hard, perhaps impossible, to control experimentally**.","children":["Researchers cannot assign people to grow up with particular languages, and separating the factors would take groups that differ in only one way."]}],"l2-sensitive-01":[],"l2-sensitive-02":[{"text":"**L2 learning**, meaning second-language learning, works as a **model system** for sensitive period research, which is a common and easily studied situation used to answer questions about a process that is hard to study directly.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967)** proposed that the sensitive period had to do with the development of **cerebral dominance**, in which one hemisphere, usually the left, becomes specialized for language.","children":["This is also described as a change in **language laterality**, which is how much language relies on one side of the brain, so the window closes once language is fixed in the left hemisphere."]},{"text":"The theory rested on a rough alignment of **brain development** with **language milestones**, and on a review of **resilience after brain injury or surgery**.","children":["Cortical neurons grow quickly for about two years and then level off, and children who lost the left hemisphere often developed language while adults did not regain it.","The theory is about the brain's **maturational state**, its stage of physical development, because a young brain can move language to other tissue and a mature, specialized brain has fewer ways to do so."]}],"l2-sensitive-03":[{"text":"Most of what is known about **L1 sensitive periods** comes from **idiosyncratic cases**, meaning cases particular to one individual, or from relatively rare phenomena.","children":["Cases such as children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere, or Genie, also involve injury, illness or abuse, so the true cause of an outcome is hard to identify."]},{"text":"In contrast, **many people learn second languages** at different times in their lives, often with **somewhat limited success**, so researchers can study large groups who started at every age.","children":[]},{"text":"Those limits are used to reason about the **timing of the sensitive period**, because if later starters reliably end up lower, the age where the decline appears could mark when the period closes.","children":["The first language complicates this, because every L2 learner already knows a language that shapes how the new one is learned."]}],"l2-sensitive-04":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** asked whether the **critical period** that **Lenneberg (1967)** proposed for first-language acquisition, running from early infancy to puberty, also extends to second-language learning.","children":["If it does, young children should learn a second language better than adults and reach higher final proficiency."]},{"text":"They compared the English of native **Korean or Chinese speakers** who had arrived in the United States at ages ranging from early childhood to adulthood, using a **grammaticality judgment task** on a wide variety of English structures.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage**, because performance was **linearly related to age of arrival up to puberty** and was **low, highly variable and unrelated to age of arrival** after puberty.","children":["The effect appeared on every structure tested and was not an accidental result of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification.","The authors concluded that the critical period **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**."]}],"l2-sensitive-05":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** devised a **grammaticality judgment test (GJT)**, in which participants listened to recorded sentences and said whether each one was grammatical, with **no time limit**.","children":["A grammaticality judgment is a decision about whether a sentence follows the rules of the language."]},{"text":"Each ungrammatical sentence breaks one rule of English, so each example set tests one rule.","children":["“The farmer bought two pig at the market” tests **plural marking**, and “The little boy is speak to a policeman” tests the **progressive -ing** form of the verb.","“When Sam will fix his car?” tests **wh-question** word order, and “What they sell at the corner store?” tests the need for the helping verb “do” in a question."]},{"text":"Three of the sets test articles and other **determiners**.","children":["“Tom is reading book in the bathtub” is missing a needed article, and “A boys are going to the zoo this Saturday” has a determiner that does not agree with the noun in number.","“Larry went the home after the party” has an article in a spot where English allows none."]}],"l2-sensitive-06":[{"text":"The **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data relate each participant's grammar score to **age of arrival (AOA)**, and they are split at puberty, the end of the critical period that Lenneberg (1967) proposed.","children":["A **correlation** measures how closely two variables are related, and a strong negative one means that one variable falls steadily as the other rises."]},{"text":"Among **early arrivals**, who came as children, the **correlation is strongly negative**, so scores fall steadily with each later year of arrival.","children":["Those who arrived youngest score near the maximum, and age of arrival predicts most of the score in this group."]},{"text":"Among **late arrivals**, who came after puberty, the **correlation is weak**, and scores are lower overall and widely scattered.","children":["Some adults score nearly as well as older child arrivals while others who arrived at the same age score far lower, so age of arrival predicts little among adults."]}],"l2-sensitive-07":[{"text":"The **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data have three main features.","children":["**Age of arrival is strongly related to accuracy**, with earlier arrival going with higher scores.","**Early learners**, up to about age eight, are often **near-perfect**, which means native-like."]},{"text":"Late arrivals have a **nearly random distribution of accuracy**, which means that among adults age of arrival and score are only weakly related.","children":["Late arrivals still scored well above chance, but two adults who arrived at the same age could end up far apart."]},{"text":"Children who arrived after about age eight reach steadily lower levels, so the **decline begins well before puberty**.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-08":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** argue that the data are consistent with a **sensitive period** for L2 learning, which they call a **“critical period,”** for two reasons.","children":[]},{"text":"First, **ultimate attainment**, the final level a learner reaches, depends on age of arrival, which is consistent with a **loss of language-learning ability over development**.","children":["The decline starts around age seven or eight and not at puberty, so the timing does not entirely fit what Lenneberg (1967) proposed, but he was working with a large gap in the data between early childhood and adolescence."]},{"text":"Second, **attainment in late acquisition is all over the place**, so they argue that after the period closes attainment depends on **idiosyncratic learning abilities**, such as memory, motivation or analytical skill, and not on the usual language acquisition processes.","children":["Children share one natural acquisition process and so reach similar levels at similar ages, while adults must lean on their own abilities, which differ a great deal between people."]}],"l2-sensitive-09":[{"text":"The **“critical period” hypothesis** has two versions, and both say that early in life humans have a **superior capacity for acquiring languages**.","children":["On the **exercise hypothesis**, the capacity disappears or declines only if it is not exercised early, so a person who learned an L1 normally should learn an L2 as well as a child does.","On the **maturational state hypothesis**, the capacity disappears or declines with maturation whether or not it was used, so even a person with a normal L1 should learn an L2 less well as an adult."]},{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** see their data as more consistent with the **maturational state hypothesis**, because every participant had a normal first language and later starters still learned the L2 less well.","children":[]},{"text":"The L1 data cannot separate the two versions, because in cases like Genie the capacity was never exercised and both versions predict poor learning.","children":["Only L2 learners have already exercised the capacity before learning another language."]}],"l2-sensitive-10":[{"text":"**Test design** is a methodological issue, because the **grammaticality judgment task is very different from fluent language use**, where people speak and understand quickly without stopping to think about rules.","children":["The worry is that people use **metalinguistic** or **explicit knowledge**, which is conscious knowledge about language, such as knowing that English plurals add “-s.”"]},{"text":"**Ellis (2005)** contrasts **implicit knowledge** with **explicit knowledge**.","children":["Implicit knowledge is intuitive, **procedural**, variable but systematic, reached by **automatic processing**, used in fluent performance, **nonverbalizable**, and potentially only learnable **within the critical period**.","Explicit knowledge is conscious, **declarative**, often anomalous and inconsistent, reached by **controlled processing**, used when a speaker is planning or having difficulty, **verbalizable**, and learnable at **any age**."]},{"text":"An untimed GJT lets people draw on explicit knowledge, so it may miss the implicit knowledge that the critical period is supposed to affect.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-11":[{"text":"One suggestion is that the task demands of the **untimed GJT** make it a better measure of **explicit learning of “metalinguistic” knowledge** than of grammar acquisition.","children":[]},{"text":"**Ellis (2005)** compared five tests on their design features.","children":["**Imitation**, **oral narrative** and the **timed GJT** rely on feel and are done under time pressure, so they require no metalinguistic knowledge.","The **untimed GJT** and the **metalanguage** test rely on rules and have no time pressure, so metalinguistic knowledge can be used, and only imitation and oral narrative focus on meaning and not on form."]},{"text":"Only two relationships with background measures were significant.","children":["**Starting age** was related to the **timed GJT**, and **years of formal instruction** were related to the **untimed GJT** with ungrammatical items.","Formal instruction teaches explicit rules, so an age effect found with an untimed GJT may partly reflect taught knowledge and not natural grammar acquisition."]}],"l2-sensitive-12":[{"text":"**Transfer**, which means using first-language knowledge while learning a second language, is both a methodological and a theoretical issue.","children":["Languages that are **typologically more similar**, meaning similar in structure such as word order and word forms, can benefit more from transfer.","Cognates and loan words such as French **auto**, **rivière** and **bicyclette** and Japanese **korokke** and **monoreru** give learners familiar words to build on."]},{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** tested people with **English as L2** and **Chinese or Korean as L1**, languages that differ greatly from English, so these learners could get little help from transfer.","children":[]},{"text":"If the languages were more similar, learners could reuse much of what they know, and their scores might relate to age differently.","children":["The age pattern would then depend on the particular languages involved as well as on age."]}],"l2-sensitive-13":[{"text":"**Not all aspects of the grammar are equally difficult**. **Johnson and Newport (1989)** compared errors on twelve types of English rules across native speakers and groups who arrived at different ages.","children":["The rule types are determiners, plurals, **verb subcategorization** (which kinds of words or phrases may follow a verb), past tense, pronouns, particles, auxiliaries, third-person singular, wh-questions, yes/no questions, word order and progressive -ing.","Native speakers and the youngest arrivals make very few errors of any type, while the latest arrivals struggle most with **determiners** and **plurals**."]},{"text":"Some of this looks like the **order in which children learn language**, because **progressive -ing** and **word order** are easiest for late learners and are among the first structures children master.","children":[]},{"text":"Some could be **transfer**, because **Chinese has no determiners**, so its speakers have no L1 category for “a” and “the.”","children":["Children learn **questions** very late, yet questions are among the easiest structures for late learners, who may bring general cognitive skills or L1 knowledge to them."]}],"l2-sensitive-14":[{"text":"**Birdsong and Molis (2001)** repeated the Johnson and Newport (1989) study with **Spanish speakers learning English** and found almost the reverse pattern.","children":["For Chinese and Korean speakers, the correlation with age of arrival was strong among early arrivals and weak among late arrivals.","For Spanish speakers, it was **weak among early arrivals**, who scored near the top regardless of age, and **strong among late arrivals**, who declined steadily with age."]},{"text":"**Maturational constraints** are limits on learning caused by the brain's development, and a fixed maturational schedule should give the same pattern for every first language.","children":[]},{"text":"The pattern changed with the first language instead, so the age effect depends on the **language pairing** as well as on age.","children":["Spanish is much closer to English than Chinese or Korean, so **transfer** may help Spanish speakers reach native-like grammar even when they arrive late in childhood."]}],"l2-sensitive-15":[{"text":"These findings may not even count as “conclusions,” but overall **L2 attainment is lower for older learners**.","children":[]},{"text":"How much lower depends on **what constructions are tested**, **how they are tested** and the **language pairing**.","children":["Determiners are far harder for late learners than word order, timed and untimed tests can disagree, and Spanish speakers differ from Chinese or Korean speakers in their age patterns."]},{"text":"Several things are **hard to dissociate**.","children":["**Age of acquisition** goes together with the amount and quality of experience, and **transfer effects** are mixed with what was learned in L2 acquisition.","**Competence** is underlying knowledge and **performance** is how well it is used on a task, so a score measures performance and may not reveal competence directly."]}],"l2-sensitive-16":[],"review-54":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** explains interactions between L1 and L2 in terms of **spreading activation**, which means that activating one representation partly activates the representations linked to it.","children":["L1 words are strongly linked to concepts and L2 words are linked more weakly."]},{"text":"When the task of **Marian and Spivey (2003)** is done in L2, the concept for “speaker” **partially activates the Russian word for “matches,”** spichki, because the two words begin with the same sounds.","children":["This activity **feeds back to the conceptual activation** for “matches,” which leads to **eye movements toward the L1 competitor**."]},{"text":"When the task is done in L1, the **conceptual links with L2** might be **too weak** to create this kind of interference reliably, so interference runs from the first language into the second and not reliably the other way.","children":[]}],"review-1":[],"review-2":[{"text":"A **speech continuum** is a series of sounds that change step by step along one controlled acoustic property.","children":["Here **F1 is kept constant** and the **F2 onset frequency**, where the second formant begins its transition, is **varied linearly** from the first stimulus to the last.","Because the change is linear, **adjacent stimuli differ by the same amount across the entire continuum**."]},{"text":"Along the series the F2 transition goes from rising, to nearly flat, to falling as the onset climbs past the vowel frequency.","children":[]},{"text":"Every step is physically equal, so the continuum fairly tests whether perception follows the acoustics or the categories.","children":["A purely acoustic listener would hear every step as the same size, while a categorical listener would hear steps across a boundary as different and steps within a category as alike."]}],"review-3":[{"text":"An **ideal observer model** predicts how a listener would respond under a particular account, and this one assumes perception is **non-categorical**, tracking the acoustic signal gradually with no sharp boundaries.","children":["It is a baseline for the real labeling data of **Liberman and colleagues (1957)**."]},{"text":"Its first prediction is that **categorization gets “blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**, where a **prototype** is the clearest example of a category.","children":["The endpoints are /b/ and /g/ with /d/ in the middle, and **stimulus ambiguity scales with the distance from the prototype**.","The second stimulus would be heard as /b/ about three quarters of the time and as /d/ about one quarter of the time."]},{"text":"**Labeling** would change smoothly, with responses for one category fading out in a straight slope while the neighboring category fades in, so two labels are equally likely halfway between prototypes.","children":[]}],"review-4":[{"text":"The ideal observer can also assume that perception is **linear**, so equal acoustic differences are perceived as equally large, and this predicts that **discrimination performance should be independent of labeling**.","children":["**Labeling** asks a listener to identify a sound as /b/, /d/ or /g/, while **discrimination** asks only whether two sounds are “same” or “different.”"]},{"text":"Labeling **has to have a “peaked” function**, because there are three categories and listeners are choosing between labels, so each category’s responses rise toward its prototype and fall away.","children":[]},{"text":"Every discrimination pair is two steps apart with the same physical separation, so if perception of differences in F2 onset were constant over the whole continuum, **discrimination accuracy would also be constant**.","children":["A flat pattern would mean boundaries play no role in hearing differences, and a peaked pattern would mean boundaries make some differences easier to hear."]}],"review-5":[{"text":"**Liberman and colleagues (1957)** had listeners label each stimulus on the continuum from /b/ through /d/ to /g/ and judge pairs that were one, two or three steps apart, and the results are **categorical perception**.","children":[]},{"text":"Labeling had **sharp changes**, because the first few stimuli were heard as /b/ almost every time and responses then switched almost completely to /d/, and later just as quickly to /g/.","children":["The narrow region where the label flips is the **category boundary**, and these switches are far more abrupt than the gradual slopes the ideal observer predicted."]},{"text":"**Listeners were best at discriminating sounds near the category boundaries**, while pairs inside one category were discriminated poorly, close to **chance**, the level expected from guessing.","children":["Larger steps raised accuracy overall, but the peaks stayed at the boundaries.","Discrimination predicted from labeling alone captured much of the pattern, but real accuracy was often a little higher, especially within categories, so listeners keep some sensitivity beyond the labels."]}],"review-6":[{"text":"The bear and pear eye-tracking result of **McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** is one piece of evidence for **cascaded processing**. A thresholded model can hardly explain why **the number of looks to the other member of the minimal pair increases gradually as you approach the category boundary**.","children":["A **minimal pair** is two words that differ in a single sound, such as bear and pear."]},{"text":"In a thresholded model, graded feature activity **never reaches the phoneme level**, so every token placed in a category sends the **same finished phoneme** to the word level.","children":["Word-level competition would then be identical for every /b/, which leaves nothing to produce gradually changing competitor looks."]},{"text":"A cascaded account explains the effect directly, because more support for the competing phoneme passes on more support for its word.","children":[]}],"review-7":[{"text":"The **TRACE** model also includes **feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer**, so activity runs up from phonemes to words and back down from words to phonemes.","children":["**Lexical feedback** means that an active word sends support back to its own phonemes, so a word that an uncertain sound partly activates can strengthen the reading of that sound."]},{"text":"Influence therefore travels **in both directions**, because acoustic evidence changes which words are active and active words shift the balance between possible phonemes.","children":[]},{"text":"**Feedback is different from cascading**, because cascading sends incomplete evidence forward while feedback sends influence from words back down to phonemes.","children":["An interactive model can have both, and experiments in which **word knowledge changes how sounds are heard**, such as the **Ganong effect**, are the reason to add feedback."]}],"review-8":[{"text":"One prediction for the experiment of **Ganong (1980)** comes from a **late “correction” process**, in which **categorical perception functions as usual, but there is a bias to report having heard a word**.","children":["The listener first categorizes the sound with no help from word knowledge and then leans toward the real word at the response stage."]},{"text":"This account predicts a **general reporting bias** across **word-nonword** continua, where the voiced end forms a word, **nonword-word** continua, where the voiceless end forms a word, and **neutral** continua.","children":["A bias added after perception pushes responses toward the word by the **same amount everywhere**, so even a clearly voiceless sound would sometimes be reported as voiced when the voiced version makes a word."]},{"text":"The **lexical effect**, which is the difference in voiced responses between the two lexical conditions, is therefore the **same size at every step** of the continuum.","children":[]}],"review-9":[{"text":"A second possibility is that **word knowledge shifts the categorical boundary**, so it changes where the listener switches from one phoneme category to the other.","children":["Near an ambiguous onset the interpretation that forms a real word gets extra support, which changes categorization itself and not only the reported answer."]},{"text":"This account predicts that the lexical conditions differ **only in the middle** of the continuum, because at both ends the sound is clear and every condition gives the same answer.","children":[]},{"text":"The **lexical effect** should form a **peak**, since it is **strongest for the items closest to the category boundary**, where lexical information can help resolve some ambiguity about the initial phoneme.","children":["A **flat** effect points to a reporting bias, while a **peaked** effect points to a shift in the boundary itself."]}],"review-10":[{"text":"The results of **Ganong (1980)** are strikingly consistent with the **second model**, the **boundary-shift** account, because the **lexical effect** is small at the endpoints and largest near the boundary.","children":["A peak at the boundary **rules out a simple reporting bias**, because word knowledge matters most where the acoustic evidence is weakest."]},{"text":"Ganong raised a number of possibilities for how this could happen, focusing on different kinds of **guessing strategies**, which would let listeners use word knowledge only when unsure of the sound.","children":[]},{"text":"Later models would emphasize the potential role of **feedback** in these shifts.","children":["In **interactive models**, an active word sends support back to its own phonemes, so the whole word can shape how an ambiguous part of it is interpreted."]}],"review-11":[{"text":"A **simulation of the Ganong effect in the TRACE model** tracks **phoneme and word activations** as the input unfolds. The input starts with a sound **ambiguous between /b/ and /p/** and continues with /l/, the vowel /ʌ/ and /g/, which together make **plug**.","children":["When only the first sound has arrived, /b/ and /p/ are both weakly active at about the same level, and word activation is very low."]},{"text":"As /l/ and the vowel arrive, several candidates gain activation together, including **plug**, **blood** and **blush**, because all of them fit the beginning heard so far.","children":[]},{"text":"When the final **/g/** arrives, it fits plug but not the endings of blood or blush, so **plug** becomes the most active word by a wide margin.","children":["At the phoneme level, **/p/ ends up more active than /b/**."]}],"review-12":[{"text":"As the input unfolds, **activity accumulates for “plug,”** especially when the **final /g/** arrives, because /g/ is highly inconsistent with **“blood”** and **“blush,”** which had also become active.","children":[]},{"text":"The TRACE model explains **word effects on phoneme category boundaries** in terms of **top-down processing**, which means that higher-level knowledge influences lower-level analysis.","children":["**Activity from the lexical level produces feedback to the phoneme level**, which gives **greater activation to the phoneme consistent with a word**, so /p/ gains extra support from plug."]},{"text":"The ambiguous beginning becomes clearer because /p/ belongs to the word that the whole sequence supports.","children":["Information that arrives at the end of a word can reach back and shape how an earlier sound is heard."]}],"review-13":[{"text":"**Syntactic explanations** of the garden path effect start from a **parser**, a mental process that builds a **syntactic tree** recording how the words of a sentence group into phrases and how the phrases relate.","children":[]},{"text":"In the correct tree for the horse sentence, the subject is a large noun phrase, or **NP**, holding “the horse” plus a reduced clause meaning “that was raced past the barn,” and the main verb phrase, or **VP**, contains only **fell**.","children":["In the failed parse, “raced past the barn” is the main VP with raced as the main verb, so fell has nowhere to attach and the parse breaks down."]},{"text":"The parser runs in a cognitive system with **limited memory** and **time constraints**, so it cannot wait for the end of the sentence before building structure, and it can keep only a limited number of structures in play at once.","children":[]}],"review-14":[{"text":"The **grammatical version** of the horse sentence is much more complex syntactically than the failed parse because it has **many more nodes**. A **node** is a point in a syntactic tree that stands for a grammatical grouping such as an NP or a VP.","children":["The correct tree needs extra nodes for the embedded clause and the silent “that” and “was,” while the failed parse only makes “raced past the barn” the main VP."]},{"text":"One way to save space is to **initially prefer smaller trees**, a strategy called **“minimal attachment,”** in which each new word is fitted into the existing structure with the fewest new nodes.","children":["Minimal attachment favors **raced as the main verb** because that analysis needs the fewest nodes, so when fell arrives and requires the larger structure, the reader has to rebuild the tree."]}],"review-15":[{"text":"**“Late closure”** is another parsing strategy in this framework, and it is a bias to **keep the current clause “open” as long as possible**, so incoming words are attached to the phrase being built.","children":[]},{"text":"In **“The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite,”** the phrase “with the dynamite” wants to associate with the big **verb phrase** starting with “blew open,” and **Frazier (1987)** contrasts two structures for it.","children":["The **preferred** structure attaches the phrase to the VP, so the dynamite is the **instrument** used to blow open the safe.","The **unpreferred** structure attaches it to the NP “the safe,” so the phrase says which safe was blown open."]},{"text":"Both attachments are grammatical, and here the preferred one is also the minimal attachment and the sensible meaning, so readers reach the right reading easily.","children":[]}],"review-16":[{"text":"Late closure is visible in a sentence that **requires the “unpreferred” parse to be sensible**, such as **“The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels.”**","children":["Jewels are a poor tool for blowing open a safe, so the sensible meaning is the safe that contained the jewels, which needs the unpreferred attachment to the noun phrase “the safe.”"]},{"text":"In careful tests of **reading time** and **eye movements**, people are **slower to read sentences where the sensible reading requires the unpreferred parse**, and the extra time appears right where the parse has to change.","children":["Readers **momentarily entertain the idea that the jewels were used to blow open the safe**, because the parser first attaches the phrase to the verb phrase. They then have to **retrace their steps** and attach it to the noun phrase."]}],"review-17":[{"text":"This explanation rests on a **parser that deals exclusively with the abstract syntactic structure** of the sentence, much like the **“lumber mill”** example from speech perception. In a lumber mill, each stage works on its own material and hands a finished product to the next stage.","children":["The parser builds structure from grammatical categories alone, without checking meaning or context, and meaning is worked out afterward."]},{"text":"The parser has **its own operating characteristics**, because it wants the **simplest tree possible**, which is **minimal attachment**, and it gets there partly by leaving clauses open as long as possible, which is **late closure**.","children":[]},{"text":"This deals with ambiguity very efficiently by **ignoring it until it becomes a problem**, since the parser builds only one likely structure and is usually right.","children":["When a later word does not fit, the structure must be revised, and that revision is the garden path difficulty."]}],"review-18":[{"text":"**“World knowledge” can be very local**, because it can come from the visual scene in front of us. **Spivey and colleagues (1995)** tested this with a **visual world** of real objects, which were a pencil, a box, an empty towel and an apple sitting on a towel.","children":["People heard **“Put the apple on the towel in the box”** and carried it out while their eye movements were recorded."]},{"text":"With **minimal attachment**, “the towel” temporarily seems to end the clause “put the apple on the towel,” so “on the towel” is taken as the **destination** when it really says **which apple** to move.","children":[]},{"text":"People **look at the empty towel while planning their movements**, as though considering it as a target, before planning a movement to the box.","children":["With the unambiguous instruction **“Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box,”** the eyes go from the apple straight to the box."]}],"review-19":[{"text":"In a **two-referent context** the display holds **two apples**, one on a towel and one elsewhere, along with the empty towel and the box.","children":["“The apple” alone no longer says which apple to move, so **“on the towel”** now does useful work by picking out one of the two."]},{"text":"Participants usually look at the apple on the towel and then at the box, and looks to the empty towel become much less common.","children":["**The sequence and the timing of eye movements**, measured against the nouns in the speech, **did not differ** between the ambiguous and the unambiguous instructions."]},{"text":"Listeners take “on the towel” as a description of the apple from the start, so the destination reading never takes hold, and the same sentence gets a different grammatical analysis because the visual context differs.","children":[]}],"review-20":[{"text":"With **two apples** in the display, it suddenly becomes **felicitous**, meaning natural and appropriate, to give more information about **which apple** is meant.","children":["With one apple there is no need to say which apple, so “on the towel” seems like extra information and is easily taken as a destination."]},{"text":"So **people do not look at the empty towel as much**.","children":["With one apple, the ambiguous instruction drew looks to the empty towel on a little over half of the trials, and with two apples it did so on far fewer trials, close to the level for the unambiguous instruction."]},{"text":"The words of the instruction were the same in both displays, so this is an effect of **referential context**, the set of objects a phrase could refer to. Referential context influences the grammatical relationship listeners assign while the sentence is still being heard.","children":[]}],"review-21":[{"text":"An attractive **dichotomy**, meaning a two-way division, describes **Wernicke's aphasia** as **“word salad”** or **“Jabberwocky,”** often with **neologisms**, while **syntax stays roughly intact**.","children":["A patient reported by **Kriendler and colleagues (1971)**, asked about his health, gave a long smooth answer that began “I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds” and never formed a clear message.","Another patient said “My wires don’t hire right” about his poor vision, and an aphasic physician used the neologisms **demaploze** and **tubaboys** when he was asked whether he was a doctor."]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** is described as **“telegraphic” speech**, with difficulty with **word order and grammatical particles** and relative **sparing of important content words**.","children":["A patient reported by **Badecker and Caramazza (1985)**, asked what brought him to the hospital, produced content words such as Dad, hospital and doctors with many pauses and without the grammatical words that would connect them."]},{"text":"The two patterns look like mirror images, because Wernicke's patients have fluent grammar with wrong words and Broca's patients have meaningful words with little grammar.","children":[]}],"review-22":[{"text":"Stated in terms of what is damaged, **Wernicke's aphasia** reflects problems with **activation, or possibly inhibition, in the lexicon**, and **Broca's aphasia** reflects **problems with grammar**.","children":["The **lexicon** is the mental store of words, **activation** makes a candidate word available, and **inhibition** suppresses competing words, so weak inhibition could let wrong words slip into speech."]},{"text":"That account is **fairly distant from how Broca described his original cases**, because his patient **Leborgne**, known as **“Tan,”** could only produce the one jargon syllable “tan” while he seemed to understand what was being said.","children":["His other senses were intact, so the loss was specific to speech, which makes it a dissociation, and a modern examination would describe it at phonetic, phonological, lexical and syntactic levels."]},{"text":"So **Broca's actual patients would not necessarily be classified as Broca's aphasics** today, because they were arguably unable to produce speech at all due to **motor control issues**.","children":["They would be classified as having **apraxia of speech**, a disorder in planning and coordinating the movements needed to speak, which can limit output severely without any loss of grammatical knowledge."]}],"review-23":[{"text":"The dichotomy is interesting partly because it lines up with a sharp distinction that late twentieth-century formal linguistics drew between **the grammar and the lexicon**.","children":["The **grammar** is the system of rules for combining words into sentences, and the **lexicon** is the store of individual words, which must be memorized."]},{"text":"The distinction starts with **Chomsky's formulation of the computational problem of language learning**. A new string is recognized as a sentence because it is **generated by the grammar** each person has internalized, and not because it matches some familiar item.","children":[]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** also argued for the **independence of syntax and semantics** with “colorless green ideas sleep furiously” and “furiously sleep ideas green colorless,” because neither has occurred in an English speaker's experience and yet **only the first is grammatical**.","children":["Both are equally unfamiliar and equally meaningless, so the difference must come from grammar as its own system.","The aphasia dichotomy maps onto this split, with Wernicke's aphasia as a disorder of the lexicon and Broca's aphasia as a disorder of the grammar."]}],"review-24":[{"text":"**“Man bites dog”** is the newspaper example of an unexpected event, and **psycholinguistic tests** are needed because ordinary conversation offers many **redundant cues**, which are separate sources of information that all point to the same meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"For “The dog bit the man,” a listener can combine DOG, BITE and MAN into the **most likely event**, with or without syntax, or rely on **canonical word order**, which is agent, then verb, then undergoer.","children":["The **agent** is the doer and the **undergoer** is the one the action happens to, and the **discourse context**, the surrounding conversation, clarifies a lot as well."]},{"text":"Broca's patients may depend on these **compensatory heuristics**, which are mental shortcuts that make up for a weakness elsewhere, so they should be tested with sentences like “The dog that the man is biting is angry.”","children":["In that sentence the **man** does the biting even though the dog comes first and dogs are more likely biters, so only grammatical analysis gives the right answer."]}],"review-25":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** devised a list of exactly this kind of sentence, with four groups that change how much meaning helps the listener.","children":["**Control sentences** are simple and in canonical order, such as “The girl is kicking a green ball.”"]},{"text":"The other three groups are **center-embedded**, which means a relative clause sits in the middle of the main clause, so the first noun is acted upon and the doer is inside the clause.","children":["With **semantic constraints**, as in “The apple that the boy is eating is red,” meaning alone reveals who does what, because apples cannot eat boys.","In **reversible** sentences such as “The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” either participant could do the action, and in **improbable** sentences such as “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,” common sense points to the wrong answer."]},{"text":"A listener who relies on meaning should succeed on the control and semantically constrained sentences and fail on the reversible and improbable ones, while a listener who parses normally should succeed on all four.","children":[]}],"review-26":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** used a **sentence-picture matching task**, in which people heard a sentence and chose which of the pictures matched it, so accuracy reveals who the listener thinks did what to whom.","children":["They compared healthy controls with Broca's, conduction and Wernicke's aphasia patients on the four sentence types."]},{"text":"**Healthy controls** were about equally good at all sentence types, while **Wernicke's patients were poor overall**, even on simple control sentences, probably because of their general comprehension deficits.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca's patients** did well when meaning supplied the answer but had particular difficulty keeping **“who did what to whom”** straight when **syntax conflicted with common sense**, as in the reversible and improbable sentences.","children":["This specific comprehension deficit was taken as evidence that **Broca's aphasia impacts syntax specifically**.","Their paper calls it a dissociation of algorithmic and heuristic processes, because Broca's patients lose the **algorithmic** process of grammatical analysis and keep the **heuristic** shortcuts based on meaning."]}],"review-27":[{"text":"There are **problems with this dichotomy**, and they start with a task in which **Dick and colleagues** had participants listen to **four types of sentences** and indicate **which animal was doing the action**.","children":["A dog and a cat were on a screen, and the event was always a dog biting a cat while the sentence structure changed."]},{"text":"Two types put the doer first, the **active** “The dog is biting the cat” and the **subject cleft** “It’s the dog that is biting the cat,” so choosing the first animal mentioned gives the right answer.","children":["A **cleft** sentence splits a simple sentence with “It’s” and “that” to put focus on one part."]},{"text":"Two types put the receiver first, the **object cleft** “It’s the cat that the dog is biting” and the **passive** “The cat is bitten by the dog,” so the first-animal shortcut fails.","children":["Only a listener who parses the grammar succeeds on all four types."]}],"review-28":[{"text":"When aphasia patients and **healthy aging controls** do this task, a pattern emerges much like the one **Caramazza and Zurif (1976)** observed.","children":["Elderly controls are nearly perfect on all four sentence types, and **Wernicke's** patients score low on every type, near chance."]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia patients have particular difficulty with the syntactically complex stimuli**, which are the object clefts and passives, and these are **noncanonical** structures that depart from the usual doer-first order.","children":["Their performance is **overall not so great**, because even on simple active sentences they are far below the controls, so a general drop combines with an extra cost for complex structures."]},{"text":"The other patient groups also drop on complex sentences. **Anomic** patients, whose main problem is word finding, stay fairly accurate on actives and subject clefts and drop on object clefts and passives, and **conduction** aphasia patients fall sharply on object clefts.","children":[]}],"review-29":[{"text":"The same task was given to **healthy undergraduates** under conditions that **stress the language system**, such as speeding up the speech, adding noise, or asking people to remember lists of numbers as a secondary task.","children":["**Compressed** speech is played faster than normal, a **low-pass filter** removes the higher frequencies so speech sounds muffled, and the digit lists use up memory resources."]},{"text":"Accuracy stayed high on actives and subject clefts, but **object cleft and passive sentences were particularly vulnerable to all the manipulations**, and the drop grew larger when two stressors were combined.","children":["With muffled speech that was also sped up, accuracy on object clefts and passives fell to about two thirds."]},{"text":"These students have no brain damage, so stressing the processing system is enough to produce an aphasia-like profile, which suggests that the comprehension deficits in Broca's aphasia may be a **“performance” issue after all**.","children":["Complex structures may demand more processing resources, so patients may keep their grammatical knowledge but lack the resources to use it efficiently."]}],"review-30":[{"text":"**Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked whether the comprehension deficits of Broca's aphasics are **language-specific** by having patients **match an action to an object**.","children":["In the **non-linguistic** version a drawing has a boy pantomiming an action such as licking, and patients choose the matching object from pictures such as an ice cream cone, a cake, broccoli and a rooster.","In the **linguistic** version the action was given in words instead of by pantomime."]},{"text":"Controls were nearly perfect in both versions, while aphasic patients made more errors in both and somewhat more in the linguistic version, so **Broca's aphasics had difficulty whether the stimuli were linguistic or not**.","children":["Patients made the most errors when the wrong choice, called the **distractor**, was **semantic**, meaning related in meaning to the correct object, and fewer with unrelated or **affordance-based** distractors, where an **affordance** is an action an object allows."]},{"text":"A purely grammatical deficit predicts good performance on the nonverbal version, so the difficulty there points to a broader problem with action meanings that is shared with language.","children":[]}],"review-31":[{"text":"Performance in the **non-verbal version** of the action-matching task from **Saygin and colleagues (2004)** was **significantly correlated with damage to Broca's area**.","children":["Damage linked to deficits in **non-linguistic pantomime interpretation** clustered in the left frontal region in front of the **central sulcus**, which includes Broca's area."]},{"text":"The task only asked patients to interpret a pantomimed action and choose an object, so they did not have to produce a sentence or work out the grammar of a spoken instruction.","children":[]},{"text":"Damage to Broca's area therefore affects **more than syntax**, because patients with damage there can struggle to connect an action with its meaning even when no language is involved.","children":["Any account of Broca's area has to explain both the grammatical comprehension findings and this nonlinguistic difficulty with actions."]}],"review-32":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** is measured through behaviors that change when an infant notices something new, so neither method asks the infant to name or produce a sound.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, used in Janet Werker's infant studies lab, an infant learns to turn toward a sound when it changes, because a correct head turn earns an interesting visual reward such as a lit-up moving toy.","children":["Once the link is learned, a head turn after a sound change means the infant **heard the change**."]},{"text":"In **high-amplitude sucking**, the infant sucks on a pacifier attached to a pressure recorder, and strong sucks make a sound play.","children":["As the same sound repeats, the infant grows used to it, which is **habituation**, and sucking slows.","If a new sound makes sucking **increase again**, the infant has noticed the change."]}],"review-33":[{"text":"**Eimas and colleagues (1971)** tested categorical perception in **four-month-old infants** with **high-amplitude sucking**, using syllables that varied in **voice onset time**, or **VOT**, the delay between the release of a stop consonant and the start of vocal fold vibration.","children":[]},{"text":"Infants heard one syllable until sucking slowed, and then one of **three conditions** followed.","children":["In one, the sound changed by a fixed amount of VOT and **crossed an adult phonemic category boundary**, and in another it changed by the same amount but stayed in the **same category**.","In the **control** condition the same sound continued throughout."]},{"text":"Sucking **jumped back up** only after the change that crossed the boundary, while it showed little recovery after the within-category change and kept declining in the control.","children":["The two changes were matched in acoustic size, so infants were responding to **crossing the category boundary**, and the control establishes what happens with repetition alone."]}],"review-34":[{"text":"The result from **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** was a big deal because categorical perception was considered the **first step in turning speech into a symbolic format** for later linguistic processes to use.","children":["A symbolic format stores a sound as a category, such as /b/."]},{"text":"**Four-month-olds are really little**, with gross motor milestones such as manipulating objects with two hands and holding the head and chest up while prone.","children":["Their color vision and general visual acuity are still \"in progress,\" and they are just starting to produce vowel-like sounds instead of creaky whines."]},{"text":"This is **very, very early in development** for an apparently language-specific ability to emerge, so it **seemed like evidence for innateness**.","children":["Speech categorization ran far ahead of other abilities, so it looked built in instead of learned through long experience."]}],"review-35":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing** is the decline in sensitivity to speech contrasts that the language a child hears does not use.","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees (1984)** tested **English-learning infants** on a **Hindi contrast** between two \"ta\"-like sounds and a **Salish contrast** between \"ki\" and \"qi\", counting how many infants at each age reached a discrimination criterion.","children":["At around six months most infants discriminated both contrasts they had never heard, fewer did a couple of months later, and only a small minority did by the end of the first year.","Hindi-learning and Salish-learning infants discriminated their own native contrast nearly every time, so the decline **depends on language experience**."]},{"text":"Narrowing is related to categorical perception because infants are forming native categories, and sounds that their language groups together become harder to tell apart.","children":["A nonnative distinction that falls inside one native category comes to be heard as a **within-category difference**."]}],"review-36":[{"text":"In a **very compelling narrative**, speech perception depends on **categorical perception abilities** that are a **special property of the language system** and are present very early in development.","children":[]},{"text":"Children start off as **\"citizens of the world,\"** able to hear **all contrasts**, which means they have **innate, language-general knowledge** about speech that applies to the sounds of every language.","children":["They then **rapidly tune their perceptual systems** to the specific language in their environment."]},{"text":"The **role of learning** is then to **select** among the many innately specified phonetic contrasts and narrow the **inventory**, the full set of contrasts the child can perceive, to those meaningful in the native language.","children":["Learning works by elimination, because it drops the contrasts the native language does not use."]}],"review-37":[{"text":"**The inductive problem of early word learning** is that a single experience fits many possible meanings, where **induction** means reasoning from particular examples to a general rule.","children":["A new word heard while watching a cat might mean **CAT**, **ANIMAL** or **PET**, the name **FELIX**, a property such as **FURRY** or **ALIVE**, an action such as **WALKING**, or a part such as **WHISKERS** or **LEG**."]},{"text":"All the candidate meanings make up the **hypothesis space**, and every candidate fits what the child sees, so nothing in one scene singles out the intended meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"This is a version of **Quine’s “gavagai” problem**, in which a linguist hears a speaker of an unknown language say “gavagai” as a rabbit runs by.","children":["The word might mean rabbit, undetached rabbit parts, a stage of a rabbit or something else.","Children still learn words fast, because joint attention, intentional cues and default strategies narrow the possibilities."]}],"review-38":[{"text":"Children acquire word meanings partly through **joint attention**, which is **sharing an attentional focus with another person**.","children":["Two people look at the same object, and each knows that the other is attending to it too."]},{"text":"**Gaze following**, which means looking where someone else is looking, **emerges around nine months of age**, so the infant follows an adult’s eyes to an object.","children":[]},{"text":"Gaze following **signals an understanding of reference**, because the infant grasps that the adult’s attention is aimed at something specific.","children":["If the adult then says a word, the shared object becomes the most likely referent, so joint attention shrinks the **hypothesis space** by tying the word to what the speaker is attending to."]}],"review-39":[{"text":"**Baldwin and colleagues** found that **joint attention optimizes word learning**, using two groups of infants who heard a novel word while playing with an object.","children":["In the **joint attention group**, an **adult interacting with the baby** labeled the object the baby was playing with.","In the **no joint attention group**, a **voice from behind a screen** labeled the object the baby was playing with."]},{"text":"The groups matched in that the word came while the infant was focused on the same object, so they differed only in whether a visible person was engaged with the infant.","children":[]},{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds learned the novel word in the joint attention group but not in the no joint attention group**.","children":["Hearing a word while seeing an object was not enough, because infants needed evidence that a speaker meant the word to refer to that object."]}],"review-40":[{"text":"Children narrow the **hypothesis space** for new words partly through **intentional cues from the speaker**, which are signals of what the speaker means to refer to, such as **eye gaze direction**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Baldwin** taught new words to sixteen-to-seventeen-month-olds and eighteen-to-nineteen-month-olds under two conditions.","children":["In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the **toy the infant was focused on** while saying the new word, so both cues pointed to one toy.","In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a **toy in a bucket**, a different toy from the infant’s, while saying the new word."]},{"text":"The discrepant condition separates the infant’s focus from the speaker’s focus, so it tests whether infants treat the **speaker’s gaze as evidence** of meaning.","children":["Infants who attach a word to whatever they are looking at would link it to their own toy, while infants who follow gaze would link it to the toy in the bucket."]}],"review-41":[{"text":"Intentional cues also include **affective and behavioral cues**, where affective cues are the speaker’s emotional expressions and behavioral cues are the speaker’s actions.","children":["An expression can reveal whether an object is the one the speaker was looking for, and an action such as reaching can reveal what the speaker wants."]},{"text":"**Akhtar and colleagues** found that **infants used adults’ facial expression to identify the intended referent of a novel word**.","children":["The adult’s emotional reaction to each object signaled whether it was the one meant, so infants linked the new word to the object that the expression picked out."]},{"text":"Several objects may be present when a word is spoken, and the speaker’s reaction can **single out one of them**.","children":["Satisfaction signals that the speaker found the wanted object, and disappointment signals that an object is not the one meant."]}],"review-42":[{"text":"Children also bring **word learning strategies**, which are **default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation**, where a default is a starting assumption that holds until evidence says otherwise.","children":["These strategies **narrow the hypothesis space for new words**, so children have a place to start when other cues are weak."]},{"text":"The **whole object assumption** leads a child to expect a new word to name **a whole object**, not one of its parts, its color, its texture or an action.","children":["A child who hears a new word while looking at a cat will favor “cat” over “whiskers,” “furry” or “walking.”"]},{"text":"The assumption **rules out many unlikely meanings at once** and fits the way adults usually name objects for children.","children":["It is only a starting preference, so children still learn words for parts and properties once the evidence points that way."]}],"review-43":[{"text":"**Mutual exclusivity**, from **Markman (1989)**, is the default assumption that **objects can have only one name**, and its implication is that **new words map to nameless objects**.","children":["It **emerges during the second half of the second year**."]},{"text":"Suppose a child knows the word “dog” but has no word for a parrot, and both animals are in view.","children":["The child takes a new word to mean the parrot because the dog already has a name, and the child makes this choice without any gaze or emotional cue."]},{"text":"Mutual exclusivity works with the **whole object assumption**, which says that a new word names an object, by adding that the object has no name yet.","children":["It is a default, so later learning can override it when an object turns out to have more than one name."]}],"review-44":[{"text":"**L2 learning**, meaning second-language learning, works as a **model system** for sensitive period research, which is a common and easily studied situation used to answer questions about a process that is hard to study directly.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967)** proposed that the sensitive period had to do with the development of **cerebral dominance**, in which one hemisphere, usually the left, becomes specialized for language.","children":["This is also described as a change in **language laterality**, which is how much language relies on one side of the brain, so the window closes once language is fixed in the left hemisphere."]},{"text":"The theory rested on a rough alignment of **brain development** with **language milestones**, and on a review of **resilience after brain injury or surgery**.","children":["Cortical neurons grow quickly for about two years and then level off, and children who lost the left hemisphere often developed language while adults did not regain it.","The theory is about the brain's **maturational state**, its stage of physical development, because a young brain can move language to other tissue and a mature, specialized brain has fewer ways to do so."]}],"review-45":[{"text":"Most of what is known about **L1 sensitive periods** comes from **idiosyncratic cases**, meaning cases particular to one individual, or from relatively rare phenomena.","children":["Cases such as children recovering from surgery that removed a hemisphere, or Genie, also involve injury, illness or abuse, so the true cause of an outcome is hard to identify."]},{"text":"In contrast, **many people learn second languages** at different times in their lives, often with **somewhat limited success**, so researchers can study large groups who started at every age.","children":[]},{"text":"Those limits are used to reason about the **timing of the sensitive period**, because if later starters reliably end up lower, the age where the decline appears could mark when the period closes.","children":["The first language complicates this, because every L2 learner already knows a language that shapes how the new one is learned."]}],"review-46":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** asked whether the **critical period** that **Lenneberg (1967)** proposed for first-language acquisition, running from early infancy to puberty, also extends to second-language learning.","children":["If it does, young children should learn a second language better than adults and reach higher final proficiency."]},{"text":"They compared the English of native **Korean or Chinese speakers** who had arrived in the United States at ages ranging from early childhood to adulthood, using a **grammaticality judgment task** on a wide variety of English structures.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals had a **clear and strong advantage**, because performance was **linearly related to age of arrival up to puberty** and was **low, highly variable and unrelated to age of arrival** after puberty.","children":["The effect appeared on every structure tested and was not an accidental result of experience with English, motivation, self-consciousness or American identification.","The authors concluded that the critical period **extends its effects to second-language acquisition**."]}],"review-47":[{"text":"The **Johnson and Newport (1989)** data have three main features.","children":["**Age of arrival is strongly related to accuracy**, with earlier arrival going with higher scores.","**Early learners**, up to about age eight, are often **near-perfect**, which means native-like."]},{"text":"Late arrivals have a **nearly random distribution of accuracy**, which means that among adults age of arrival and score are only weakly related.","children":["Late arrivals still scored well above chance, but two adults who arrived at the same age could end up far apart."]},{"text":"Children who arrived after about age eight reach steadily lower levels, so the **decline begins well before puberty**.","children":[]}],"review-48":[{"text":"The **“critical period” hypothesis** has two versions, and both say that early in life humans have a **superior capacity for acquiring languages**.","children":["On the **exercise hypothesis**, the capacity disappears or declines only if it is not exercised early, so a person who learned an L1 normally should learn an L2 as well as a child does.","On the **maturational state hypothesis**, the capacity disappears or declines with maturation whether or not it was used, so even a person with a normal L1 should learn an L2 less well as an adult."]},{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** see their data as more consistent with the **maturational state hypothesis**, because every participant had a normal first language and later starters still learned the L2 less well.","children":[]},{"text":"The L1 data cannot separate the two versions, because in cases like Genie the capacity was never exercised and both versions predict poor learning.","children":["Only L2 learners have already exercised the capacity before learning another language."]}],"review-49":[{"text":"**Allopenna and colleagues (1994)** found the **cohort effect**, where a **cohort** is the set of words that share the same beginning sounds.","children":["Participants viewed a grid of pictures and shapes and followed spoken instructions, with a **beaker** as the target, a **beetle** that shares its beginning, a **speaker** that rhymes with it and an unrelated **carriage**."]},{"text":"Looks to the beaker and the beetle **rose together early**, because both fit the first sounds of the word, and looks to the beetle fell once the sounds that distinguish the two words arrived.","children":["Looks to the speaker rose later and stayed smaller because its overlap comes at the end of the word, and looks to the carriage stayed near zero."]},{"text":"The cohort effect is this **early competition** from words that share a beginning with the target, so words compete as soon as they match the incoming sounds and drop out when the input stops matching them.","children":[]}],"review-50":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** examined the cohort effect with **cross-language competitors**, which are objects whose name in the other language begins like the spoken target word.","children":[]},{"text":"They hypothesized that if **L1 words are always activated**, native Russian speakers should look at a book of matches when the target is “speaker” in English.","children":["English **speaker** begins with the same sounds as Russian **спички (spichki)**, which means matches, so the matches should draw looks if Russian is active during English listening.","The two words are tied only by **sound**, so looks to the matches during an English task directly measure activation of spichki."]},{"text":"Each English target came with an English competitor and a Russian competitor, as when **speaker** came with **spear** and **spichki**, or **marker** with **marbles** and **marka**, stamp.","children":["For Russian targets the roles were reversed, so Russian **spichki** came with English **spear** and Russian **spitsi**, knitting needles."]}],"review-51":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** first had Russian-English bilinguals follow instructions in English, which tests **competition during L2 spoken word recognition**.","children":[]},{"text":"When only one competitor was present, participants made eye movements to **within-language and between-language competitors to the same extent**, and far more often than to an unrelated control object.","children":["The Russian name of an object pulled the eyes as strongly as a similar English word did."]},{"text":"When both competitors were present, participants looked at the within-language competitor more, but they **still looked at the between-language competitor** about twice as often as in the control condition.","children":["The first language stays active during second-language listening, so bilinguals still choose the correct object while L1 words compete along the way."]}],"review-52":[{"text":"The same bilinguals in the study by **Marian and Spivey (2003)** then did the experiment in Russian, which tests **competition during L1 spoken word recognition**.","children":["A Russian within-language competitor still drew extra looks, so the cohort effect appeared in Russian."]},{"text":"**English words do not seem to be activated** when native Russian speakers do the task in Russian.","children":["An object with a similar English name drew only slightly more looks than the control object, and that small numerical difference is **not statistically significant**, which means it falls within ordinary chance variation."]},{"text":"The two experiments together give an **asymmetric** pattern, because Russian words became active during English tasks and English words did not become reliably active during Russian tasks.","children":["The asymmetry fits the idea that the first language is more firmly established and more easily activated than the second."]}],"review-53":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** explains interactions between L1 and L2 in terms of **spreading activation**, which means that activating one representation partly activates the representations linked to it.","children":["The model has an L1 lexicon, or vocabulary, an L2 lexicon and a store of concepts, with **lexical links** joining the two lexicons and **conceptual links** joining each lexicon to the concepts."]},{"text":"The **L1 lexicon** is assumed to be **larger** and **more strongly connected to relevant concepts**, so L2 words have weaker links to their meanings.","children":[]},{"text":"The **L2 lexicon** is assumed to have **strong forward connections to the L1 lexicon**, so activation can spread more readily from an L2 word to an L1 word than the other way.","children":["This occurs because this direction is emphasized in learning, since learners usually attach each new L2 word to its L1 translation."]}],"review-55":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** of **Kroll and Stewart (1990, 1994)** makes a **counter-intuitive prediction** even for **balanced bilinguals**, people who are about equally skilled in both languages.","children":["They do not look like “two monolinguals in one,” with each language running separately."]},{"text":"For a balanced bilingual the **conceptual links are similarly strong for both languages**, so each concept strongly activates its word in both languages.","children":["**Competitive dynamics**, in which words from the two languages keep competing for selection, and **inhibition**, the suppression of the word from the language not in use, will be engaged all the time."]},{"text":"This prediction has led to a lot of work on the advantages of bilinguals in non-linguistic **cognitive control** tasks, where cognitive control is the ability to manage attention and suppress irrelevant responses.","children":["Constant practice at selecting one language and suppressing the other might improve control of attention in general."]}]}};
